// Spoken answers (60-90 s) for networking guide
(window.SPOKEN = window.SPOKEN || {})['networking'] = {
  1: {
    a: `The OSI model has seven layers: physical, data link, network, transport, session, presentation, and application. As a platform engineer I operate mostly at layers three to seven, but knowing all of them helps me localise a fault. Layer one is cables, NICs, and signals, rarely my problem in the cloud. Layer two is MAC addresses, switches, VLANs, and ARP. Layer three is IP addressing and routing, where ping, routes, and security groups matter. Layer four is TCP and UDP, ports, and connection state, where firewalls, load balancers, and kube-proxy operate. Layers five to seven are sessions, encoding, TLS, and application protocols like HTTP and DNS. The model is useful as a troubleshooting ladder: if I can't ping an address, I look at layers one to three; if I can reach the host but the port is refused or times out, layer four; if TCP connects but TLS fails, certificates and protocol versions; if TLS works but the API returns errors, the application. It also maps to tools: an L4 load balancer routes on IP and port, an L7 one inspects HTTP. In practice the TCP/IP model is what's implemented, but OSI gives shared vocabulary, so I can say this is an L7 problem and be understood.`,
    f: ["How do you use the layers as a troubleshooting ladder?","Where do load balancers and firewalls sit in the model?"]
  },
  2: {
    a: `TCP is connection-oriented and reliable: it does a three-way handshake, guarantees ordered delivery with acknowledgements and retransmission, and provides flow and congestion control. UDP is connectionless: no handshake, no acknowledgements, no ordering, just fire and forget, so it has lower latency and overhead but no delivery guarantees. I choose TCP when correctness matters: HTTP APIs, databases, file transfers, SSH, anything where lost or reordered data would break the application. I choose UDP when speed and timeliness matter more than perfection, or when the application handles reliability itself: DNS queries, which are tiny request-response exchanges, real-time voice and video, gaming, metrics and logging with tolerable loss, and QUIC, which builds reliable streams on top of UDP to avoid TCP head-of-line blocking. Operationally, ss shows TCP and UDP listeners separately, and the failure modes differ: TCP problems show as retransmissions, resets, and timeouts; UDP loss is silent, so I need application-level monitoring. Firewalls and load balancers must be configured for the right protocol, and UDP is harder to load balance and troubleshoot, since there's no connection state to inspect. UDP can also be abused for amplification attacks, so I'm careful exposing UDP services publicly.`,
    f: ["Why does DNS mostly use UDP?","How does QUIC get reliability over UDP?"]
  },
  3: {
    a: `The TCP three-way handshake establishes a connection: the client sends a SYN, the server replies with SYN-ACK carrying its own sequence number, and the client sends an ACK; then data flows. When a connection closes, the side that closes first ends up in TIME_WAIT for about sixty seconds. It exists so that delayed packets from the old connection don't corrupt a new connection using the same address and port tuple, and so the final ACK can be retransmitted if lost. It matters in production because a client making many short connections accumulates TIME_WAIT sockets, and since each outbound connection to the same destination needs a unique source port, it can run out of ephemeral ports, giving cannot assign requested address errors. I count them with ss, check the port range, and look at tcp_tw_reuse. The right fix is to reuse connections with keep-alive and connection pooling, and widen the port range or spread across more source or destination addresses; enabling tw_reuse for outbound helps. The old tw_recycle was removed because it breaks clients behind NAT. Also relevant: SYN floods target the handshake, mitigated with SYN cookies and backlog tuning, and handshake latency adds round trips for every new connection, a reason to keep connections alive.`,
    f: ["Why does TIME_WAIT exist?","What is the right fix for ephemeral port exhaustion?"]
  },
  4: {
    a: `ARP, the Address Resolution Protocol, maps IP addresses to MAC addresses within a local network segment, at layer two. When a host wants to send to an IP on the same subnet, it broadcasts who has this IP; the owner replies with its MAC, and the sender caches the mapping. For off-subnet destinations it ARPs for the default gateway. I inspect the cache with ip neigh and send probes with arping. Problems ARP can cause: stale or wrong entries after an IP moves to a new machine, causing traffic to go to the old MAC until the cache expires, which matters in failover with virtual IPs, solved with a gratuitous ARP announcement; duplicate IP addresses, where two hosts answer, giving intermittent connectivity; ARP spoofing or poisoning, an attack that redirects traffic through an attacker, mitigated by dynamic ARP inspection and port security; ARP storms or table exhaustion in large flat networks, which is one reason to keep layer-two domains small; and incomplete entries when the target is unreachable. In the cloud, ARP is handled by the virtual network, but it can still appear in on-premises, VM, and container bridge troubleshooting. Flushing the cache can clear a stale entry, used carefully.`,
    f: ["Why is a gratuitous ARP used in failover?","How can you detect a duplicate IP address?"]
  },
  5: {
    a: `NAT, Network Address Translation, rewrites addresses in packets so private hosts can share public addresses. Source NAT changes the source address, used for outbound internet access from private networks, like a home router or a cloud NAT gateway. Destination NAT changes the destination address, used for port forwarding and by load balancers, and Kubernetes services use DNAT to reach pod IPs. Masquerade is a dynamic form of SNAT that uses the outgoing interface's current address, handy when the public IP changes. Port address translation, PAT, maps many private addresses to one public address by using different source ports, which is what most NAT does. NAT is stateful: the device keeps a table of translations, so return traffic is mapped back. Operational consequences: the NAT table and port space can be exhausted, causing connection failures under heavy outbound traffic to the same destination; the source IP seen by a remote partner is the NAT address, so allow-lists must include it, and egress IPs should be stable, which cloud NAT gateways with elastic addresses provide; NAT breaks protocols that embed addresses, and complicates inbound connections; it adds a hop to troubleshoot, so conntrack tools help. IPv6 largely removes the need for NAT, though cloud designs still use NAT64 and egress controls.`,
    f: ["Why does NAT port exhaustion happen?","Why do partners need to allow-list your NAT IP?"]
  },
  6: {
    a: `When a client needs to resolve a name, it first checks local caches: the application, the OS resolver cache, and the hosts file. If there's no answer, it asks its configured recursive resolver, such as the ISP's, a corporate server, or the cloud provider's. The recursive resolver checks its own cache, and on a miss it walks the hierarchy. It asks a root server, which refers it to the TLD servers, like dot com; the TLD servers refer it to the domain's authoritative name servers; and the authoritative server returns the actual record. The resolver caches the result for the record's TTL and returns it to the client. Along the way there can be CNAME chains, which add more lookups, and the transport is usually UDP on port 53, falling back to TCP for large responses. For production, TTL is the key: low TTLs let you move traffic quickly but increase query load, and because every layer caches, changes are never instant. In Kubernetes, CoreDNS adds another layer, with search domains and ndots affecting how many queries a lookup triggers.`,
    f: ["Why can ndots cause slow DNS in Kubernetes?","How would you debug an intermittent DNS failure?"]
  },
  7: {
    a: `For intermittent DNS failures I debug systematically along the resolver path. First, dig the name and read the status and flags and the query time, to see whether it's a SERVFAIL, NXDOMAIN, timeout, or just slow. Then bypass the local resolver by querying a known public resolver, to see whether the problem is my resolver or the authoritative data; dig with trace walks the delegation chain from the root to find a broken or lame delegation. Check resolv.conf for nameservers, search domains, and options; with systemd-resolved, resolvectl status shows the actual state. Intermittent patterns suggest one bad server among several, so I query each nameserver directly several times. Check whether the failures correlate with UDP versus TCP fallback for large responses, with DNSSEC validation, with rate limiting, or with load on the resolver. In Kubernetes, check CoreDNS pod health and scaling, the conntrack UDP race, and ndots expansion generating many queries. Packet capture on port fifty-three shows unanswered queries and retries. Fixes: remove or repair the bad nameserver, add a local caching resolver, tune ndots and timeouts, scale the resolver, fix the zone or delegation, and make applications cache results. Monitor DNS latency and failure rate as a dedicated indicator.`,
    f: ["Why query each nameserver individually?","What does dig +trace show?"]
  },
  8: {
    a: `DNS TTL is how long resolvers may cache a record. A long TTL reduces load and latency but slows propagation of changes; a short TTL makes changes take effect quickly but increases query load and dependence on DNS availability. For zero-downtime changes, I plan the TTL. Several hours, at least one old TTL, before the planned change, I lower the TTL to something like sixty seconds. I wait for the old, high-TTL cached entries to expire across the internet. Then I make the DNS change, and verify propagation by querying several public resolvers, and test the new endpoint. Keep the old endpoint running until traffic has drained, because some resolvers and clients ignore TTL, cache longer, or keep long-lived connections. After the change is confirmed stable, I restore a higher TTL. Caveats: some clients, including JVMs by default in some configurations, cache DNS indefinitely or for a long period, so applications need sensible settings; negative caching of missing records can delay new records; and during the change window I should monitor traffic to both old and new. For failover records, keep the TTL low permanently, with health checks. For migrations, an alternative is to avoid DNS cutover altogether by switching at the load balancer.`,
    f: ["Why lower the TTL well before the change?","Why might some clients ignore the TTL?"]
  },
  9: {
    a: `Authoritative DNS servers hold the actual zone data and give definitive answers for their domains; they don't go looking elsewhere. Recursive resolvers work on behalf of clients: they receive a query, walk the hierarchy from the root to the TLD to the authoritative server, and cache the results to speed up later answers. I can test an authoritative server directly with dig and the no-recursion flag, and find a domain's authoritative servers with an NS query. Split-horizon DNS, or split-brain DNS, serves different answers for the same name depending on who asks, typically internal clients get private IP addresses and external clients get public ones. This is common with a private hosted zone alongside a public zone, so internal services resolve to internal endpoints while the public name points to a load balancer. It's useful for security, performance, and avoiding hairpin routing. Pitfalls: inconsistent records between the views cause hard-to-debug it works from here but not there problems; VPN clients and containers may use the wrong resolver; caching and search domains complicate it; and zone changes must be made in both views. To troubleshoot, I query from each network location and compare, and check which resolver and view is answering.`,
    f: ["How do you find which nameservers are authoritative for a domain?","What problems does split-horizon DNS introduce?"]
  },
  10: {
    a: `They point to different layers. Connection refused means the packet reached the destination host and it actively rejected it by sending a TCP reset, typically because nothing is listening on that port, or a firewall rule is set to reject. That tells me the network path works and the problem is the service: it's down, listening on a different port or interface, or bound to localhost only. Connection timed out means I got no response at all, so the SYN was silently dropped or never answered. Typical causes are a firewall or security group dropping packets, a routing problem, the host being down or unreachable, or an overloaded server with a full accept queue. To tell them apart and debug, I use curl or nc with verbose output, then check from the server with ss to see whether something is listening on the right address and port. For timeouts I work along the path with traceroute, security groups, network ACLs, and route tables, and use tcpdump to see whether the SYN arrives and whether a SYN-ACK goes back.`,
    f: ["Why would a security group cause a timeout rather than a refusal?","What does a full accept queue look like from the client side?"]
  },
  11: {
    a: `I count sockets by state with ss and read what's abnormal. TIME_WAIT is the state after a graceful close by the side that closed first; many of them are normal in high-throughput services and indicate high connection churn. The risk is source port exhaustion for clients making many short connections to one destination; the fix is connection pooling and keep-alive, a wider port range, and tcp_tw_reuse for outbound. CLOSE_WAIT is different and usually a bug: the remote side closed the connection, and my application hasn't closed its end. The socket stays in CLOSE_WAIT until the application calls close, so a large and growing number means a connection or file descriptor leak in the application, such as not closing responses, missing finally blocks, or a pool that isn't releasing connections. It eventually causes too many open files. The fix is in the code: ensure sockets are closed in all paths, and I identify the owning process with ss and lsof. Other states worth knowing: SYN_RECV in large numbers suggests a SYN flood or backlog problem; FIN_WAIT2 piling up means the peer isn't closing; ESTABLISHED in large numbers means real concurrency or idle leaked connections. I monitor state counts as metrics to detect leaks early.`,
    f: ["Why does CLOSE_WAIT indicate an application bug?","What do many SYN_RECV sockets suggest?"]
  },
  12: {
    a: `Flow control and congestion control are two different mechanisms in TCP. Flow control protects the receiver: it advertises a window size in the TCP header telling the sender how much data it can accept; if the application reads slowly, the window shrinks, possibly to zero, and the sender pauses. Congestion control protects the network: the sender infers congestion from packet loss or rising round-trip time and adjusts its congestion window, starting slowly and growing, then backing off on loss. Algorithms include CUBIC, the Linux default, which is loss-based, and BBR, which models bandwidth and round-trip time and performs better on lossy or long-distance links. They matter for cloud services because throughput on a connection is limited by the window divided by round-trip time, so high-latency paths between regions need larger buffers and window scaling; loss triggers slowdowns that can dramatically reduce throughput; new connections start in slow start, which is another reason to reuse connections; and zero-window events indicate a slow consumer, an application problem, not a network one. I inspect with ss TCP info to see the congestion window, RTT, and retransmits, and with netstat statistics. Tuning options include buffer sizes, choosing BBR for wide-area transfers, and enabling window scaling.`,
    f: ["What limits TCP throughput on a long-distance link?","How do you spot a slow consumer using TCP information?"]
  },
  13: {
    a: `Subnetting divides an IP range into smaller networks, expressed in CIDR notation where the number after the slash is the count of network bits. The number of addresses is two to the power of thirty-two minus the prefix length. A slash twenty-four has two hundred fifty-six addresses, with two hundred fifty-four usable in traditional networking, as the network and broadcast addresses are reserved; cloud providers reserve a few more, for example five per subnet in AWS. A slash twenty-five has one hundred twenty-eight; slash sixteen has sixty-five thousand five hundred thirty-six, a common VPC size; and slash twenty has four thousand ninety-six, a common subnet size. Each bit added to the prefix halves the range. To check whether two addresses are in the same subnet, apply the mask and compare the network portions. In practice I plan ranges so that VPCs, subnets, and Kubernetes pod and service ranges don't overlap across environments, because overlaps block peering and VPN connections; leave room for growth; allocate by tier and availability zone; and document in an IP address management system. Tools like ipcalc or Python's ipaddress module do the arithmetic. Kubernetes with pod-per-IP networking can consume addresses quickly, so size pod subnets generously.`,
    f: ["Why are cloud subnets' usable addresses fewer than 2^n minus 2?","Why avoid overlapping CIDR ranges across environments?"]
  },
  14: {
    a: `IP routing is hop by hop. When a host sends a packet, it compares the destination with its own subnet. If it's on the same subnet, it delivers directly: ARP for the destination's MAC and send the frame. If not, it sends the packet to its default gateway, again using ARP for the gateway's MAC while keeping the destination IP unchanged. The gateway looks up the destination in its routing table, choosing the most specific matching route, the longest prefix match, and forwards to the next hop, decrementing the TTL, and rewriting the layer-two header at each hop. This repeats until the packet reaches a router directly connected to the destination subnet. The routing table is populated by connected networks, static routes, and dynamic protocols such as BGP and OSPF. I inspect with ip route, and ip route get shows exactly which route, interface, and source address would be used for a destination. Troubleshooting points: a missing or wrong default route, asymmetric routing where the return path differs and a stateful firewall drops the reply, overlapping routes, and the TTL expiry shown by traceroute. In the cloud, route tables attached to subnets decide whether traffic goes to an internet gateway, NAT gateway, peering, or a transit gateway.`,
    f: ["What is longest prefix match?","What is asymmetric routing and why does it break stateful firewalls?"]
  },
  15: {
    a: `BGP, the Border Gateway Protocol, is the path-vector routing protocol that exchanges routes between autonomous systems and runs the internet. It runs over TCP port one seven nine. iBGP runs within one autonomous system; eBGP runs between different ones, used for internet routing and cloud connectivity. Routers advertise prefixes along with attributes such as the AS path, local preference, and MED, and choose the best path by policy and path attributes, rather than pure shortest distance. In cloud networking it's used for hybrid connectivity: Direct Connect, ExpressRoute, and Cloud Interconnect use BGP sessions to exchange on-premises and VPC routes dynamically; site-to-site VPNs often use BGP for dynamic failover between tunnels; transit gateways and cloud routers use it; and Kubernetes CNIs like Calico use BGP to advertise pod CIDRs to the network. Benefits: automatic failover when a link goes down, and route filtering for control. Operational care: filter what you advertise and accept, since a misconfiguration can leak routes or blackhole traffic, as happened in well-known internet outages; set prefix limits; use route priorities and AS path prepending to steer traffic; monitor session state and received prefixes; and use BFD for fast failure detection.`,
    f: ["What does the AS path attribute do?","How is BGP used for hybrid cloud failover?"]
  },
  16: {
    a: `Traceroute lists the routers along a path by sending packets with increasing TTLs and noting which router replies with time exceeded. Hops showing stars don't necessarily mean a problem: the router may filter or rate-limit ICMP time exceeded responses, or deprioritise them, while still forwarding traffic fine. The key question is what happens after the stars: if later hops respond, the earlier hop is just hiding; if the trace stops there and never reaches the destination, something may be dropping traffic. I interpret by comparing: use TCP-based traceroute to the real port, which behaves like the application's traffic and gets past ICMP and UDP filters, and use mtr for continuous per-hop loss and latency. Loss that starts at a hop and persists to the end is real; loss at a single middle hop that disappears later is rate limiting. A trace that stops at a particular hop consistently, and the application also fails, points to a firewall, route, or outage at or after that hop. Action: gather evidence, run it from both directions since return paths may differ, identify the network owner, whether my cloud VPC, my provider, or the destination, and engage them with the mtr output. Also check security groups, routes, and ACLs on my side first.`,
    f: ["Why use TCP traceroute rather than the default?","How do you read mtr loss correctly?"]
  },
  17: {
    a: `After the TCP handshake, the HTTPS handshake negotiates encryption. In TLS 1.2 the sequence is: the client sends ClientHello with supported protocol versions, cipher suites, and the server name via SNI; the server replies with ServerHello choosing the parameters, and sends its certificate; the client verifies the certificate chain against trusted CAs, checks expiry, and that the hostname matches; then a key exchange, usually ephemeral Diffie-Hellman, which gives forward secrecy; both sides derive symmetric session keys and exchange finished messages; then application data flows encrypted. TLS 1.3 streamlines this to one round trip, and supports zero-RTT resumption for repeat connections. SNI lets one IP serve many certificates. ALPN negotiates HTTP/2 or HTTP/1.1 during the handshake. Why it matters operationally: the handshake adds latency, a few round trips, which is a reason for connection reuse and session resumption; certificate problems, expired, wrong hostname, missing intermediates, or an untrusted CA, are a common cause of failures; protocol or cipher mismatches cause handshake failures; and TLS termination at a load balancer versus end-to-end changes where certificates must be managed. I debug with openssl s_client, which shows the chain and negotiated parameters, and curl verbose.`,
    f: ["What does SNI do?","How does TLS 1.3 reduce handshake latency?"]
  },
  18: {
    a: `HTTP status codes are grouped by class. Two hundred codes mean success: 200 OK, 201 Created, 204 No Content. Three hundred codes are redirects: 301 permanent, 302 temporary, 304 not modified for caching. Four hundred codes are client errors: 400 bad request, 401 unauthenticated, 403 forbidden, 404 not found, and 429 rate limited. Five hundred codes are server-side: 500 internal error, 502 bad gateway, 503 service unavailable, 504 gateway timeout. For triage, the code and who generated it point to the layer. A 502 from a proxy or load balancer means it got an invalid response or the connection to the backend failed, so the backend crashed, closed the connection, or the keepalive timeouts are mismatched. A 503 usually means no healthy backends, overload, or deliberate load shedding. A 504 means the backend didn't respond within the proxy's timeout, so it's slow. A 500 is typically an application bug or failing dependency. 4xx surges can mean a bad client release, auth system trouble, or an attack. Also check which component emitted the code, by looking at response headers and access logs at each layer. Curl with headers shows quickly. For monitoring, alert on 5xx rates and latency, not 4xx alone, but watch for sudden 4xx changes.`,
    f: ["What does a 502 versus a 504 tell you?","Why check which component generated the error?"]
  },
  19: {
    a: `HTTP/1.1 uses one request at a time per connection, since pipelining is unreliable in practice, so browsers open several parallel connections; it suffers head-of-line blocking, and sends headers as uncompressed text on every request. HTTP/2 multiplexes many concurrent streams over a single TCP connection, compresses headers with HPACK, supports server push and stream prioritisation, and uses a binary framing layer. Benefits: fewer connections and handshakes, better use of bandwidth, lower latency for pages with many resources, and it's negotiated via ALPN in the TLS handshake, so it effectively requires HTTPS in browsers. Caveats: it still runs on TCP, so a single lost packet blocks all streams, TCP-level head-of-line blocking, which HTTP/3 solves by using QUIC over UDP. Operationally: load balancers and proxies must support it; gRPC requires HTTP/2, and long-lived multiplexed connections can cause uneven load distribution at layer four, since all requests from one client go to one backend, so use layer-seven balancing; connection-level limits, such as maximum concurrent streams, matter. Old optimisations like domain sharding and sprite sheets become unnecessary or counterproductive. I check with curl, which shows the protocol used, and with openssl s_client for the ALPN result.`,
    f: ["Why does gRPC need layer-7 load balancing?","What problem does HTTP/3 solve that HTTP/2 doesn't?"]
  },
  20: {
    a: `For TLS certificate errors, I first inspect the actual certificate presented using openssl s_client with the server name, and view the chain, validity dates, subject alternative names, and issuer. Common causes: expired certificate, the most frequent, especially when auto-renewal silently failed; hostname mismatch, where the name isn't in the SAN list; an incomplete chain, a missing intermediate certificate, which works in browsers that fetch intermediates but fails in programmatic clients; an untrusted or private CA not in the client's trust store; a wrong certificate served because SNI isn't sent or the load balancer's default certificate is used; clock skew on the client making a valid certificate look expired; revoked certificates; and protocol or cipher mismatch. Intermittent errors suggest several backends with different certificates, so I test each IP. Curl with the insecure flag helps separate certificate problems from network problems, but only for diagnosis. Resolution: renew or reissue, fix the chain by deploying the full chain file, update trust stores, correct the SAN list, and reload the service so it picks up the new certificate. Prevention is automation and monitoring: ACME with cert-manager or managed certificates, expiry alerts at thirty, fourteen, and seven days, and synthetic checks that verify the full chain.`,
    f: ["Why do some clients fail on a missing intermediate that browsers tolerate?","How do you detect expiring certificates before they break something?"]
  },
  21: {
    a: `A stateless firewall evaluates each packet independently against rules on source, destination, port, and protocol; it doesn't remember connections, so to allow a reply you need an explicit rule for the return traffic, including ephemeral port ranges. A stateful firewall tracks connection state, so when an outbound connection is allowed, the return traffic is automatically permitted, and unsolicited inbound traffic is dropped. Linux iptables with conntrack is stateful, with a rule accepting established and related packets; cloud security groups are stateful, whereas network ACLs, such as AWS NACLs, are stateless and need both directions. Stateful firewalls are simpler to write and safer by default, but they consume memory for the connection table, which can be exhausted under floods, and can cause trouble with asymmetric routing, where the reply returns via a different path and the firewall has no state for it. Stateless filters are fast and suit high-throughput edge cases or coarse subnet-level controls. In practice I combine: security groups for per-instance stateful rules, NACLs as a coarse extra layer, and for deeper inspection, application-aware or next-generation firewalls and WAFs. Troubleshooting tip: a connection that works one way but not the other often points to a stateless rule missing return ports.`,
    f: ["Why do NACLs need rules for return traffic?","How can asymmetric routing break a stateful firewall?"]
  },
  22: {
    a: `DDoS attacks aim to make a service unavailable by overwhelming it, and they come in three types. Volumetric attacks flood bandwidth, such as UDP or ICMP floods and amplification through DNS or NTP. Protocol attacks exhaust state in network devices and servers, like SYN floods that fill connection tables. Application-layer attacks exhaust CPU or backend resources with seemingly legitimate requests, like HTTP floods and slowloris. Protection works in layers. At the edge, use the provider's DDoS protection, such as AWS Shield, Cloud Armor, or a CDN, which absorb volumetric attacks by their sheer capacity, with anycast distributing traffic. Use a WAF with rate limiting, bot detection, geo and IP reputation rules, and challenge pages for application-layer floods. On the origin side: don't expose origin IPs directly, restrict them to the CDN or load balancer ranges; enable SYN cookies and tune backlogs; use autoscaling carefully with cost limits; cache aggressively so floods hit the cache, not the backend; apply per-client rate limits and timeouts; and design expensive endpoints to be authenticated or queued. Prepare a runbook: how to identify the attack pattern, who to call at the provider, how to enable stricter rules, and how to communicate. Test with game days and monitor traffic baselines to detect anomalies early.`,
    f: ["Why hide the origin IP behind a CDN?","How do application-layer attacks differ from volumetric ones?"]
  },
  23: {
    a: `In regular TLS only the server presents a certificate, and the client verifies the server's identity. In mutual TLS both sides present certificates and verify each other, so the server also cryptographically knows who the client is. This gives strong service-to-service authentication and encryption in transit without passwords or tokens, and a basis for zero-trust: a request is trusted because of its verified identity, not its network location. In Kubernetes, mTLS is usually implemented by a service mesh such as Istio or Linkerd: a sidecar proxy, or in newer designs a node-level proxy, is injected next to each workload and handles the TLS; the application talks plain HTTP to its local proxy, and proxies establish mTLS between themselves. The mesh control plane acts as a certificate authority, issuing short-lived certificates to each workload based on its service account identity and rotating them automatically. Policies, like Istio's PeerAuthentication, set strict mode, which requires mTLS, or permissive mode, useful during migration, and authorisation policies specify which identities may call which services. Operational concerns: certificate rotation and CA root management, the overhead of proxies, debugging encrypted traffic, interoperability with non-mesh workloads, and gradual rollout from permissive to strict. Without a mesh, cert-manager and application-level TLS can do it, with more work.`,
    f: ["What identity does a mesh certificate carry?","Why start with permissive mode when rolling out mTLS?"]
  },
  24: {
    a: `A forward proxy sits in front of clients and sends their requests to the internet: the clients are hidden from the servers. It's used for egress filtering, policy enforcement, caching, and auditing of outbound traffic, and clients are configured to use it, such as through proxy environment variables. A reverse proxy sits in front of servers: clients talk to it as if it were the origin, and it forwards requests to backends, hiding the backends from the clients. Typical uses of reverse proxies: TLS termination, load balancing, caching, compression, routing by host or path, authentication, rate limiting, and WAF functions; nginx, Envoy, HAProxy, and cloud load balancers play this role, as do ingress controllers in Kubernetes. The distinction is about who it represents: forward proxies represent the client, reverse proxies represent the server. Operational points: proxies change what the backend sees, so I need X-Forwarded-For or the PROXY protocol to preserve client IPs; timeouts and keepalives must be coordinated between the proxy and backends to avoid 502 errors; proxy environment variables, including no-proxy lists, are a frequent source of why it works from my laptop but not from the server issues; and a proxy is also a single point of failure and a choke point that needs redundancy and monitoring.`,
    f: ["Which proxy type hides the client and which hides the server?","Why do proxies need X-Forwarded-For?"]
  },
  25: {
    a: `Load balancers distribute requests across backends using different algorithms. Round robin goes through backends in sequence and works well when requests are similar and backends are uniform. Least connections sends to the backend with the fewest active connections, better when request cost varies or connections are long-lived. Weighted variants give proportional shares, useful for mixed instance sizes and canary releases, like ninety percent to the stable version and ten to the new one. IP hash maps a client's address to a backend, giving a form of affinity without cookies. Consistent hashing on a key such as a user or cache key keeps the same requests going to the same backend and minimises disruption when backends change, which is valuable for caches. Least response time or power-of-two-choices pick better-performing backends, and the latter is simple and effective at avoiding hot spots. Beyond the algorithm, health checks matter most: unhealthy backends must be removed quickly, and slow start avoids overwhelming new ones. Other considerations: connection draining on deploys, layer four versus layer seven, how stickiness interacts with balance, and how the load balancer itself scales and is made redundant. I pick the algorithm based on request characteristics and verify with metrics on per-backend load.`,
    f: ["When is least connections better than round robin?","Why is consistent hashing good for caches?"]
  },
  26: {
    a: `Session affinity, or sticky sessions, routes requests from the same client to the same backend, using client IP hash, a cookie inserted with the backend ID, or a header. It's used when the application keeps session state in memory on the server, so moving the user to another backend would lose the session, or to improve cache locality, or for protocols like WebSocket where the connection naturally sticks. The risks: uneven load, since heavy users or clients behind one NAT IP all hit one backend, creating hotspots; failover pain, since if the sticky backend dies, those sessions are lost; harder deployments and scaling, because draining a backend means waiting for sessions, and new backends receive no traffic from existing users; and it hides poor design. The better approach is to make the application stateless: store sessions in a shared store like Redis or a database, or use signed tokens such as JWTs, so any backend can serve any request, which gives better resilience, even load distribution, and simpler scaling. If I must use affinity, I prefer cookie-based with a limited lifetime over IP hash, set sensible TTLs, plan for backend loss, and monitor per-backend load. For WebSockets and long-lived streaming, affinity is inherent, so handle reconnection gracefully.`,
    f: ["Why is IP-hash affinity unreliable behind a NAT?","How would you remove the need for sticky sessions?"]
  },
  27: {
    a: `Layer four load balancers route based on IP address and port, without looking inside the traffic. They're fast, with low latency and high throughput, protocol-agnostic, can preserve the client's source IP, and handle any TCP or UDP protocol including databases and TLS passthrough; examples are kube-proxy's iptables or IPVS rules, AWS NLB, and HAProxy in TCP mode. Layer seven load balancers terminate and parse the application protocol, usually HTTP, so they can route by host, path, headers, or cookies, rewrite requests, terminate TLS, apply authentication, rate limiting, and retries, do canary splitting, gather detailed request metrics, and integrate with a WAF; examples are nginx or Envoy ingress, AWS ALB, and the GCP HTTP load balancer. Trade-offs: layer seven costs more CPU and adds latency, and the client IP is conveyed via headers; layer four is simple and scalable but blind to content, and with HTTP/2 or gRPC long-lived connections it balances connections, not requests, leading to uneven load. I choose layer seven for web and API traffic needing content-based routing and observability, and layer four for non-HTTP protocols, extreme throughput, or when I want end-to-end TLS untouched. Often both are combined: a network load balancer in front of layer-seven proxies for scale and static IPs.`,
    f: ["Why can an L4 balancer distribute gRPC unevenly?","When would you put an L4 balancer in front of L7 proxies?"]
  },
  28: {
    a: `A VPC, virtual private cloud, is a logically isolated, software-defined network in a cloud account where I define IP ranges, subnets, route tables, gateways, and security controls. Traditional networking is built from physical switches, VLANs, hardware routers, and spanning tree, with changes needing physical or device-level configuration. A VPC is created by API in seconds and managed as code. Differences: there's no real layer two in the usual sense; the provider's fabric handles ARP and switching, so broadcast-dependent designs and tricks like gratuitous ARP failover work differently; routing is defined by route tables attached to subnets, deciding whether traffic goes to an internet gateway, NAT gateway, peering, VPN, or transit gateway; security is applied through stateful security groups per instance and stateless network ACLs per subnet; subnets are tied to availability zones, so multi-AZ designs use multiple subnets; IP addresses are partly reserved by the provider; and bandwidth, packet-per-second, and connection limits are per instance type. Design considerations: non-overlapping CIDR ranges for peering and hybrid connectivity, public versus private subnets, egress control, private endpoints to cloud services, flow logs for visibility, and segmentation by environment or account. Kubernetes adds pod networking on top, which consumes VPC addresses with some CNIs.`,
    f: ["Why must VPC CIDR ranges not overlap?","How do security groups differ from network ACLs?"]
  },
  29: {
    a: `An overlay network is a virtual network tunnelled over an underlying physical or cloud network, called the underlay. VXLAN is a common technology: it encapsulates layer-two Ethernet frames inside UDP packets, so pods on different nodes appear to be on the same layer-two segment even though the nodes are only connected by layer three. In Kubernetes, the CNI plugin implements pod networking: each pod gets an IP, and cross-node pod traffic is encapsulated by the overlay, as Flannel, Calico in VXLAN or IP-in-IP mode, Weave, and Cilium can do. The alternative is a non-overlay approach, routing pod CIDRs natively via BGP or cloud route tables, or using VPC-native addressing, which avoids encapsulation overhead. Trade-offs of overlays: they work on any network without cooperation from the underlay and simplify setup, but add header overhead, so the MTU of the pod network must be reduced or packets get fragmented or dropped, a classic cause of mysterious hangs on large transfers; they add CPU cost for encapsulation; and they make troubleshooting harder, since underlay tools see only UDP between nodes. I identify the CNI by looking at kube-system pods, inspect overlay interfaces with ip link, check MTU consistency, and use tcpdump on the node to see encapsulated traffic.`,
    f: ["Why must you lower the MTU on an overlay network?","What is the difference between overlay and native routing CNIs?"]
  },
  30: {
    a: `A service mesh moves service-to-service networking concerns out of application code into the infrastructure. A sidecar proxy, usually Envoy, is injected into every pod, and all traffic goes through it; the application talks to localhost, and the proxy handles the rest, while a control plane such as Istiod configures the proxies. Capabilities: mutual TLS between services with automatic certificate rotation; traffic management, such as retries, timeouts, circuit breaking, load balancing, canary and weighted routing, and fault injection; authorisation policies; and observability, with consistent metrics, access logs, and distributed traces for every call without code changes. The benefit is uniform policy and visibility across polyglot services. The costs: added latency and resource overhead per pod, a control plane to operate and upgrade, more moving parts to debug, such as a misconfigured sidecar causing mysterious 503s, and complexity of configuration. Newer designs, like ambient mode or eBPF-based meshes, reduce the sidecar overhead. I'd adopt a mesh when there are many services needing mTLS, consistent traffic control, and observability, and avoid it for a handful of services where simpler tools suffice. For debugging, I use proxy status, config analysis, sidecar logs, and metrics, and check that sidecars are in sync with the control plane.`,
    f: ["What problems does a mesh solve that libraries don't?","What are the drawbacks of sidecar proxies?"]
  },
  31: {
    a: `For intermittent errors in a microservice I follow the signal methodically, not random command firing. First quantify: what percentage of requests fail, on which endpoints, and since when? Second correlate: with deploys, config changes, traffic spikes, resource saturation, or upstream and downstream incidents. Third isolate where in the call chain the failure originates, using distributed traces and per-hop metrics to see which service returns the first error. Then slice the failures by every dimension: pod, node, zone, client version, and request type, since intermittent often means one bad instance or path, for example one unhealthy pod still receiving traffic. Check logs of the failing pods around the error times, restart counts, readiness status, and resource limits for OOM kills or throttling. Check the network path: DNS, connection reuse, timeouts and retries, and conntrack. Check dependencies: database connection limits, rate limits, slow queries. Look for patterns in time, such as every five minutes pointing to a cron job, garbage collection, or a cache expiry. Once I have a hypothesis, test it safely, for example by removing the suspect pod from rotation. Fix the cause, and add the alert or test that would have caught it. Keep the team updated on findings and status during the investigation.`,
    f: ["Why slice failures by pod and zone?","How do you use time patterns to find a cause?"]
  },
  32: {
    a: `To diagnose high latency between two services I first establish a baseline and then find where the time goes. ping gives round-trip time and loss, and mtr shows per-hop latency and loss over many cycles. For application-level breakdown, curl with timing variables separates DNS lookup, TCP connect, TLS handshake, time to first byte, and total, which immediately shows whether time is in name resolution, connection setup, or the server's processing. ss with TCP info for active connections shows round-trip time, retransmissions, and congestion window. If connection setup is slow, look at DNS, SYN retransmits, and TLS; if the first byte is slow, the server is slow, so use tracing to look inside; if transfer is slow, look at bandwidth, window sizes, and loss. Compare same-zone versus cross-zone or cross-region paths, since physical distance sets a floor on latency. Check for packet loss and retransmissions, which cause large tail latency through retransmission timeouts; look at CPU saturation or throttling on either end, queueing in proxies and sidecars, noisy neighbours, and connection pool waits. Use percentiles, not averages. Fixes include co-locating services, reusing connections, tuning timeouts, caching, and eliminating unnecessary hops, validated with before-and-after measurements.`,
    f: ["How does curl timing separate DNS, connect, and server time?","Why can a few retransmissions dominate tail latency?"]
  },
  33: {
    a: `To detect packet loss, I start with ping at volume to get a loss percentage, and mtr with many cycles to see per-hop loss, remembering the rule that loss starting at a hop and persisting through to the destination is real, while loss only at one middle hop is likely ICMP rate limiting. On the hosts, look at interface statistics for errors and drops, with ip minus s link and ethtool statistics for NIC-level drop counters, TCP retransmission counters from netstat or nstat, and softnet statistics for kernel backlog drops. TCP retransmits in ss indicate loss on a specific connection. Locating it: capture on both ends to see where packets disappear, check each segment: the NIC and driver, ring buffer overflows, the virtual switch or cloud network, firewalls and conntrack tables dropping, overloaded middleboxes, and congested links. Common causes: duplex mismatch or bad cables on physical networks, microbursts overflowing buffers, an overloaded firewall or NAT, MTU issues, cloud instance network limits being hit, which providers expose as allowance exceeded counters, and wireless interference. Fixes depend on the segment: replace hardware, increase ring buffers, adjust queue sizes, move to larger instances, spread traffic, or engage the provider with evidence. Monitor retransmit rates and drops continuously to catch it early.`,
    f: ["How do you tell NIC-level drops from network-level loss?","What are cloud instance network allowance counters?"]
  },
  34: {
    a: `When a service works from one location but not another, I look at what differs along the path. I run the same tests from both places: curl verbose to see where it fails, whether in DNS, connecting, TLS, or the response; dig from each to compare DNS answers, since split-horizon, geo-routing, or resolver caching may return different IPs; and traceroute, preferably TCP on the service port, to compare the network paths. Bypass DNS by connecting to a known IP with the right Host header, to separate name resolution from connectivity. Then the usual path-dependent causes: DNS returning different addresses, one of which is bad; firewall, security group, or allow-list differences, since the failing location's source IP may not be permitted; different egress NAT addresses; routing or peering differences, BGP changes; proxies configured in one location only; MTU differences, such as a VPN path; CDN or edge POP problems affecting one region; TLS inspection by a corporate middlebox; and clock or trust store differences. Capture traffic at the failing location to see whether packets leave and what returns. Once I find the difference, fix it at the right layer, and record the knowledge. Also build synthetic monitoring from multiple locations to detect this class of problem proactively.`,
    f: ["Why bypass DNS when comparing locations?","What could differ in the source IP between the two locations?"]
  },
  35: {
    a: `tcpdump is the command-line capture tool, and Wireshark is the graphical analyser. With tcpdump I choose the interface, or any, and use BPF filters to limit the capture to what I care about: a host, a port, a protocol, or TCP flags, for example only reset packets, or DNS on port fifty-three. I use minus n to avoid name resolution, write to a pcap file for later analysis, and limit size and duration or use rotation so I don't fill the disk. Then I open the file in Wireshark, or use tshark on the server, for deeper analysis: follow a TCP stream, view the handshake, see retransmissions, duplicate ACKs, zero windows, and the timing between request and response, and decode protocols including DNS and HTTP, and TLS handshake details though not encrypted payloads. I capture on both client and server to localise where packets are lost or delayed. In Kubernetes, I use an ephemeral debug container or a sniffing plugin to capture within a pod's network namespace. Reading patterns: SYN without SYN-ACK means a block or drop; SYN followed by RST means nothing is listening; repeated retransmissions mean loss; long gaps mean server delay. Be mindful of privacy and sensitive data in captures, handle them as such, and delete them when done.`,
    f: ["How do you capture traffic inside a Kubernetes pod?","What does a zero-window message tell you?"]
  },
  36: {
    a: `High kernel networking overhead shows up as CPU spent in interrupt handling instead of in applications. In top with the per-CPU view, the si column, software interrupts, is the one to watch; mpstat per CPU shows whether interrupt load is concentrated on one core, which is the typical problem when a single NIC queue is serviced by one CPU. The softnet statistics file shows per-CPU packet processing, including drops when the backlog is full and time squeezes. sar for network devices shows throughput and packets per second, since packet rate, not bandwidth, is often what overwhelms a CPU. perf can show kernel networking functions consuming time. Causes: very high packet rates with small packets; a single-queue NIC or poor interrupt distribution; a lack of receive-side scaling; excessive iptables or conntrack processing; encapsulation overhead from overlays or tunnels; or a DDoS. Fixes: enable and tune multiple NIC queues with RSS and spread interrupts across cores, using IRQ affinity or irqbalance; enable offloads such as GRO, GSO, and TSO; use larger packets where possible, jumbo frames in controlled networks; reduce firewall rule counts or use nftables sets and eBPF; scale out; and in the cloud use enhanced networking instances. Verify with before-and-after packet-per-second tests.`,
    f: ["Why is packets-per-second often more important than bandwidth for CPU load?","What does IRQ affinity do?"]
  },
  37: {
    a: `ICMP, the Internet Control Message Protocol, is the network layer's control and error-reporting protocol. Its main message types: echo request and reply, used by ping to check reachability and measure round-trip time; destination unreachable, with codes for network, host, protocol, or port unreachable and, importantly, fragmentation needed, which drives path MTU discovery; time exceeded, sent when a TTL expires, which is how traceroute works; and redirects. For diagnostics: ping tests reachability and latency; ping with a larger size and the do-not-fragment flag finds the path MTU, which is how I diagnose tunnel and VPN black holes; traceroute and mtr use TTL-expired responses to map the path. Cautions: many networks filter ICMP, so a failed ping doesn't prove a host is down, and conversely a successful ping doesn't prove the service works, so test the actual TCP port. Blocking all ICMP is a mistake, because the fragmentation-needed messages are essential for path MTU discovery, and blocking them causes hangs on large transfers. Security groups and firewalls should allow the necessary ICMP types. ICMP can also be abused for floods and tunnelling, so rate limit it instead of removing it entirely. In IPv6, ICMPv6 is even more critical since it carries neighbour discovery.`,
    f: ["Why must fragmentation-needed ICMP messages be allowed?","Why doesn't a failed ping prove a host is down?"]
  },
  38: {
    a: `MTU is the largest packet a link can carry without fragmentation, typically 1500 bytes on Ethernet. Mismatches cause mysterious problems because small packets work while large ones fail. The classic symptoms are that ping works, a TCP connection establishes, but the TLS handshake hangs or large transfers stall, since larger packets are the first to exceed the path's MTU. Normally Path MTU Discovery handles it: a router that can't forward a big packet with the don't-fragment bit set sends back an ICMP fragmentation-needed message, and the sender reduces its packet size. But if a firewall blocks ICMP, the sender never learns, and you get a black hole. Overlay networks make it worse: VXLAN or IPsec tunnels add headers, so the effective MTU inside is lower, around 1450 for VXLAN, and a pod network configured at 1500 will have trouble. To diagnose, I ping with the don't-fragment flag at varying payload sizes to find the real path MTU. Fixes are consistent MTU on the interfaces and CNI, allowing the relevant ICMP messages, or clamping TCP MSS on the gateway.`,
    f: ["Why does the TLS handshake specifically hang?","How does MSS clamping work?"]
  },
  39: {
    a: `Negative caching means resolvers cache the fact that a name doesn't exist, an NXDOMAIN response, for a period set by the SOA record's minimum value. That can cause incidents: if a service looks up a record before it has been created, say during a deployment ordering mistake or when a new service name is queried before the DNS record is added, the resolver caches the failure; then even after the record is created, clients keep getting NXDOMAIN until the negative TTL expires, so the new service seems unreachable for minutes or hours. It can also affect Kubernetes, where CoreDNS has its own cache settings and applications may cache failures. Diagnosis: dig for the name shows NXDOMAIN and the SOA record's TTL; querying the authoritative server directly shows the record exists while a cached resolver doesn't see it. Mitigation: flush the resolver caches affected, or wait; and prevent by creating DNS records before anything queries them, ordering deployment steps, setting a low SOA minimum on zones that change often, and configuring sensible negative TTLs in CoreDNS and applications. Also, applications shouldn't cache lookup failures for long, and retry logic should have backoff. It's one of those problems where DNS looks correct from the authoritative side yet clients still fail.`,
    f: ["How do you prove the record exists but a cache is stale?","What setting controls the negative TTL?"]
  },
  40: {
    a: `Socket buffers are kernel memory used to hold data waiting to be sent or received on a connection. The receive buffer holds data that has arrived but the application hasn't read yet, and the send buffer holds data waiting for transmission or acknowledgement. Their size limits TCP throughput: the amount of data in flight can't exceed the window, which is bounded by buffer size, so on high-bandwidth, high-latency paths, the bandwidth-delay product determines the buffers needed. Too small, and throughput is limited; Linux auto-tunes within the minimum, default, and maximum values in tcp_rmem and tcp_wmem, bounded by the core maximums, which I raise for long fat networks. Too large can cause problems: memory consumption across many connections, and bufferbloat, where excessive buffering in network devices or hosts creates high latency under load, since packets wait in long queues; loss-based congestion control doesn't back off until buffers fill. Mitigations: active queue management such as fq_codel or cake, BBR congestion control, and right-sizing buffers. I check actual usage with ss showing memory, and the relevant sysctls. Symptoms to recognise: low throughput on high-latency links despite free bandwidth indicates small windows; high latency under load while bandwidth is saturated indicates bufferbloat.`,
    f: ["What is the bandwidth-delay product?","How does bufferbloat harm latency?"]
  },
  41: {
    a: `ECMP, Equal-Cost Multi-Path routing, lets a router use several routes of equal cost to the same destination at once, distributing traffic across the paths instead of picking just one. Distribution is typically by hashing the flow, using source and destination IP, ports, and protocol, so all packets of one connection take the same path, avoiding reordering, while different connections spread across links. Benefits: more aggregate bandwidth, and automatic failover when a path fails, as the hash is redistributed across remaining paths. In cloud networking it appears in several places: multiple VPN tunnels or Direct Connect links advertising the same prefixes via BGP, transit gateways spreading traffic over multiple VPN connections to scale throughput past a single tunnel's limit, and the internal fabrics of cloud and data-centre networks, which use leaf-spine designs with ECMP. Caveats: a single flow is limited to a single path's bandwidth, so one big transfer doesn't benefit; hash collisions can cause uneven load, with elephant flows overloading a link; path asymmetry can confuse stateful firewalls and NAT if return traffic takes a different path; and changing the number of paths can reshuffle flows and reset connections, which resilient hashing mitigates. I verify with the routing table showing multiple next hops and test with multiple parallel flows.`,
    f: ["Why does ECMP hash by flow rather than per packet?","Why might a single large transfer not benefit from ECMP?"]
  },
  42: {
    a: `A CDN, content delivery network, caches content at edge points of presence around the world and serves users from the nearest location, reducing latency, offloading the origin, and absorbing traffic spikes and some attacks. It works by routing the user, via DNS or anycast, to a nearby edge; on a cache hit the edge serves directly, on a miss it fetches from the origin and caches according to headers. Cache behaviour is controlled by Cache-Control headers, with max-age and public to allow caching, surrogate-control for CDN-specific TTLs, and the Vary header to cache variants, for example per encoding; cache keys can include query strings, headers, or cookies. When to use: static assets like images, scripts, and video, definitely; also cacheable API responses, and as a protective layer with TLS termination, DDoS mitigation, and WAF for dynamic traffic. Pitfalls: stale content requiring invalidation or versioned filenames; accidentally caching personalised or private content because of missing headers, which is a security risk; low cache hit ratio from poor cache keys; origin shielding to avoid thundering herds at the origin; and debugging that requires looking at CDN headers and logs. Measure the hit ratio, origin load, and latency by region, and secure the origin so that traffic can only come through the CDN.`,
    f: ["How do you avoid caching personalised responses by mistake?","What is origin shielding?"]
  },
  43: {
    a: `eBPF, extended Berkeley Packet Filter, lets you run small, sandboxed programs inside the Linux kernel, verified for safety and JIT-compiled, without changing kernel source or loading modules. Programs attach to hooks such as network interfaces, system calls, and tracepoints, giving fast, programmable, low-overhead access to packets and events. It's changing cloud-native networking in several ways. For networking itself, Cilium uses eBPF to implement pod networking, service load balancing, and network policy, replacing iptables, which scales poorly because rules are evaluated linearly and updating large rule sets is slow; eBPF uses hash maps for constant-time lookups, so it handles thousands of services better, and it can bypass parts of the network stack for lower latency. For observability, eBPF gives deep visibility into connections, latency, DNS, and HTTP without sidecars or code changes, which tools like Hubble, Pixie, and Parca use. For security, it enables runtime enforcement and detection of suspicious syscalls and network behaviour, as in Tetragon and Falco. It also enables service mesh functionality without sidecars. Caveats: it needs relatively modern kernels, the tooling is complex, debugging eBPF programs requires specialised skills, and privileged access is needed. I inspect with bpftool, and adopt it where the scale, observability, or performance benefits justify it.`,
    f: ["Why does eBPF scale better than iptables for services?","What can eBPF observability do without sidecars?"]
  },
  44: {
    a: `Zero trust means never trust, always verify: no implicit trust based on network location, so every request is authenticated, authorised, and encrypted regardless of where it comes from. The traditional model trusted everything inside the perimeter, so one compromised host could move laterally. In practice I implement it in layers. Identity: strong authentication for users with SSO and MFA, and for workloads using cryptographic identities such as SPIFFE or cloud workload identity, instead of IP addresses. Encryption: mTLS between services, often via a service mesh, so both sides prove who they are. Authorisation: least-privilege policies that say which identity may call which service and method, enforced at the proxy or application, and default-deny network policies in Kubernetes so pods can only talk to what they need. Device and context: check device posture and request context for access to internal tools through an identity-aware proxy instead of a VPN that grants broad network access. Segmentation: micro-segmentation to limit blast radius. Visibility: log and monitor all access decisions, flow logs, and anomalies. Rollout is gradual: start with inventory and visibility, then default-deny in audit mode, then enforce service by service. It isn't a product but a strategy, and the trade-offs are operational complexity, certificate management, and the effort of defining policies.`,
    f: ["How does zero trust change the role of the VPN?","How would you roll out default-deny network policies safely?"]
  },
  45: {
    a: `My methodology for the application is slow in a cloud-native environment has phases. Observe: quantify slow. Which percentile, P50 or P99? Which endpoints? Compared with what baseline? What is the error rate? Scope: is it all users or some, one region, one service, one tenant, and did it start after a deploy or change? Isolate: use distributed tracing to find which hop or span owns the time, and per-service golden signals, latency, traffic, errors, and saturation, to see which component is degraded. Drill into the layer: for the network, DNS, connection setup, TLS, retransmits; for compute, CPU throttling, memory pressure, garbage collection, thread and connection pools; for storage and databases, slow queries, locks, IOPS limits; for dependencies, third-party latency and rate limits; for Kubernetes, pod scheduling, restarts, noisy neighbours, HPA lag, and node pressure. Hypothesise and test one thing at a time, using the least invasive checks. Mitigate if possible while investigating: roll back a recent change, scale out, shed load, or fail over. Verify the fix against the original metric, not just that something changed. Communicate throughout, and finish with a postmortem and prevention: SLOs, better instrumentation, and load tests. The point is structure and evidence, not a list of random commands.`,
    f: ["What do you check first when only P99 is bad?","How do traces help isolate the slow hop?"]
  },
  46: {
    a: `MTU is the largest payload a link can carry in one frame, typically fifteen hundred bytes on Ethernet. MSS is the largest TCP data segment, equal to the MTU minus the IP and TCP headers, usually fourteen hundred sixty. Path MTU Discovery finds the smallest MTU along the whole path: the sender sets the don't-fragment bit, and if a router can't forward a packet that large, it drops it and sends back an ICMP fragmentation-needed message, so the sender lowers its packet size. Tunnels, VPNs, and overlays like VXLAN add headers and reduce the effective MTU. A black hole occurs when the ICMP messages are blocked by a firewall: the sender never learns, keeps sending large packets that are silently dropped, while small packets and the handshake work. Symptoms: connections establish, small requests succeed, but large transfers or TLS handshakes with big certificates hang. I diagnose with ping using the do-not-fragment flag and varying sizes to find the working maximum, and packet captures showing retransmissions of large segments. Fixes: allow the needed ICMP, set a lower MTU on the tunnel or interface, clamp the MSS on the tunnel endpoint so TCP never sends oversized segments, and ensure consistent MTU along the path, including inside Kubernetes overlays.`,
    f: ["How does MSS clamping avoid the black hole?","How do you find the real path MTU with ping?"]
  },
  47: {
    a: `NAT rewrites IP addresses, and often ports, as packets cross a boundary. SNAT, source NAT, rewrites the source address for outbound traffic from private to public. DNAT, destination NAT, rewrites the destination for inbound traffic, mapping a public address to a private backend, as load balancers and port forwarding do. PAT, port address translation, lets many private hosts share one public address by assigning different source ports; it's what most people mean by NAT. In cloud networking: a NAT gateway provides SNAT and PAT for private subnets to reach the internet outbound, without allowing inbound connections; an internet gateway performs one-to-one NAT between an instance's private and public or elastic address; load balancers and Kubernetes services perform DNAT to reach backends and pods; and kube-proxy or the CNI uses SNAT masquerade for pod egress. NAT is stateful, tracking translations, so the state table and port space can be exhausted, producing connection failures under heavy outbound traffic to one destination; the remote side sees the NAT address, so partners allow-list that IP and egress addresses should be stable; and logs at the destination show the NAT address, not the originating workload, so correlation needs NAT or flow logs. Understanding where each happens helps debug source IP and connectivity issues.`,
    f: ["Why does a private subnet need a NAT gateway?","How do you identify which workload used a NAT address?"]
  },
  48: {
    a: `ARP maps an IP address to a MAC address within a layer-two broadcast domain, the glue between layer-three routing and layer-two delivery. A host wanting to send to an address on its subnet broadcasts who has this IP; the owner replies with its MAC; the sender caches the mapping in its ARP table with a timeout. Off-subnet traffic goes via ARP for the gateway. Gratuitous ARP is an unsolicited announcement that an IP is now at a given MAC, used when a failover moves a virtual IP to a standby, so neighbours update their caches immediately instead of waiting for entries to expire. Problems: ARP caching means stale entries after a failover or IP reassignment send traffic to the old MAC until expiry, causing a short outage unless a gratuitous ARP is sent; duplicate IPs cause intermittent connectivity as two hosts answer; ARP spoofing lets an attacker redirect traffic, mitigated by dynamic ARP inspection; and large flat layer-two networks generate ARP broadcast storms and table pressure, which is why networks are segmented. In the cloud, the virtual network handles ARP, and floating IP movement works through API calls instead of gratuitous ARP. I inspect with ip neigh, probe with arping, and flush a suspect entry carefully.`,
    f: ["Why does a failover send a gratuitous ARP?","How does ARP caching cause a short outage after a VIP moves?"]
  },
  49: {
    a: `Cloud networking maps onto OSI layers, and the layer of a component tells you what it can and can't see. Layer two, frames and MAC addresses: VLANs and the switching fabric under the VPC, which the cloud mostly abstracts. Layer three, IP and routing: VPC route tables, subnets, NAT gateways, internet gateways, transit gateways, and network ACLs and security groups matching on IP. Layer four, TCP and UDP: network load balancers, like AWS NLB, kube-proxy, and security group port rules; they see addresses and ports but not content. Layer seven, application: application load balancers, ingress controllers, API gateways, WAFs, and service meshes, which parse HTTP and can route by host, path, header, or cookie, terminate TLS, authenticate, and retry. It matters because it determines capability and failure modes. An L4 balancer can't route by URL and preserves the client IP; an L7 balancer can, but needs headers to convey the client IP and adds processing latency. A WAF only sees what an L7 component exposes, encrypted traffic passing through at L4 is invisible, and a health check at L4 only verifies the port while L7 can verify an actual endpoint. When troubleshooting, naming the layer narrows the search and tells me which tools apply.`,
    f: ["What can an L4 load balancer not do that an L7 can?","Why does the layer of a health check matter?"]
  },
  50: {
    a: `IPv6 solves IPv4 address exhaustion with a 128-bit address space, so every device can have a globally routable address and NAT isn't needed. Practical differences: IPv4 has 32-bit addresses, about four billion, and relies on NAT and ARP; IPv6 uses neighbour discovery instead of ARP, supports SLAAC autoconfiguration, link-local addresses on every interface, and has a simpler header, with no router fragmentation. ICMPv6 is essential and must not be blocked. Adoption is gradual, so most networks run dual-stack: hosts have both IPv4 and IPv6 addresses and use whichever works. The Happy Eyeballs algorithm makes dual-stack robust: the client starts connecting over IPv6 and, after a very short delay of a few hundred milliseconds, also tries IPv4, using whichever connection succeeds first, so a broken IPv6 path doesn't cause long delays. Operational points: firewalls, security groups, and monitoring must be configured for both protocols, since forgotten IPv6 rules can expose services; DNS needs AAAA records, and a wrong AAAA record causes failures that only some clients see; applications must handle IPv6 literals in logs and allow-lists; and IPv6-only with NAT64 and DNS64 is used for large environments such as Kubernetes clusters that run out of IPv4 addresses.`,
    f: ["What does Happy Eyeballs protect users from?","Why is blocking ICMPv6 harmful?"]
  },
  51: {
    a: `Conntrack is the kernel's table of every connection passing through a host that does stateful filtering or NAT, so return traffic can be matched and NAT mappings kept consistent. Each entry records the flow's tuple, protocol, and state. It's finite: the table size is set by nf_conntrack_max, and when it fills up, the kernel logs table full, dropping packet, and new connections fail while existing ones continue. That takes down busy hosts such as NAT gateways, Kubernetes nodes running kube-proxy, load balancers, and API servers handling many short connections. I check the current count against the maximum, list entries with the conntrack tool, and look at timeouts. Causes: genuine high concurrency or churn, very long established-state timeouts keeping dead entries, UDP flows like DNS creating many short entries, connection leaks, and attacks. Fixes: raise the maximum and hash table size, remembering memory cost; lower timeouts, especially the established TCP timeout; reduce connection churn with keepalive and pooling; exempt traffic that needs no tracking with raw table NOTRACK rules; and spread load across more nodes. In Kubernetes, node-local DNS caching cuts DNS entries. Monitor the usage percentage and alert early, since symptoms look like random timeouts.`,
    f: ["Why do short-lived UDP flows stress conntrack?","How do you exempt traffic from connection tracking?"]
  },
  52: {
    a: `The stateful versus stateless distinction determines whether I must explicitly allow return traffic, and it's exactly the security group versus network ACL difference in AWS. Security groups are stateful: they track connections, so if I allow an outbound request, the response is automatically allowed, and I only write rules in the direction I initiate. They are allow-only, with no explicit deny, apply at the instance or network interface level, and can reference other security groups, which is powerful for tiered architectures. Network ACLs are stateless: they apply at the subnet level, evaluate each packet independently, process numbered rules in order, can include explicit allow and deny, and need rules for both directions including ephemeral port ranges for return traffic. Typical use: security groups as the primary, fine-grained control, and NACLs as a coarse extra layer, for example to block a malicious address range at the subnet boundary. Troubleshooting implications: if a connection works one way but replies are lost, check the NACL return rules; if a subnet-wide block is needed quickly, a NACL deny is the tool; and flow logs show which layer rejected traffic. Mistakes to avoid are forgetting ephemeral ports in NACLs, rule ordering errors, and over-permissive 0.0.0.0/0 rules in security groups.`,
    f: ["Why do NACLs need ephemeral port rules?","When would you use a NACL deny rule?"]
  },
  53: {
    a: `Recursive resolution is how a name becomes an IP when nothing is cached. Take www.example.com. First the client's stub resolver asks its recursive resolver, such as a corporate or public one. If it has no cache entry, the recursive resolver asks a root server, which doesn't know the answer but refers it to the servers for the dot-com TLD. It asks a TLD server about example.com, which refers it to the domain's authoritative nameservers, found from NS records and glue records where necessary to avoid circular lookups. Then it asks the authoritative server for www.example.com and gets the answer, possibly following a CNAME chain with further lookups. The resolver caches each step according to its TTL, including the NS delegations, so later queries skip the upper levels, and returns the answer to the client, which caches it too. Cached layers include the application, the OS, and the recursive resolver. Operational relevance: a cold cache query can take several round trips, which is why resolvers matter for latency; failures at any step show up as SERVFAIL or timeouts; lame delegations and broken NS records cause intermittent errors; and dig with trace performs this walk to show where it breaks.`,
    f: ["What are glue records for?","What does dig +trace show you?"]
  },
  54: {
    a: `DNS record types each have a purpose. A maps a name to an IPv4 address and AAAA to IPv6. CNAME makes one name an alias of another name. MX lists mail servers. TXT holds arbitrary text, used for SPF, DKIM, DMARC, and domain verification. NS delegates a zone to nameservers. SOA holds zone metadata including the negative caching TTL. PTR does reverse lookups, SRV gives service host and port, and CAA restricts which CAs may issue certificates. The CNAME-at-apex problem: DNS rules say a CNAME can't coexist with other records at the same name, and the zone apex, like example.com, must have SOA and NS records, so you can't put a CNAME there. That's painful when you want the root domain to point at a cloud load balancer or CDN hostname, which gives a name instead of a fixed IP. Fixes: provider-specific alias or ANAME records, such as Route 53 alias records, which resolve to the target's IPs at the DNS level; CNAME flattening offered by some DNS providers, which resolves the target and serves A records; redirecting the apex to www at the web layer; or using static IPs, such as an anycast address, in an A record. Alias records also avoid an extra lookup and can follow health checks.`,
    f: ["Why can't a CNAME exist at the zone apex?","How do alias records differ from CNAMEs?"]
  },
  55: {
    a: `DNS can distribute and fail over traffic by returning different answers, but it's a coarse tool limited by caching. Techniques: round-robin DNS returns multiple A records and clients pick one, giving a rough spread. Weighted records send a percentage to one set of endpoints and the remainder to another, useful for canaries and migrations. Latency-based and geo routing return the nearest or best region's endpoint. Failover routing uses health checks to withdraw unhealthy endpoints, and multi-value answers return only healthy IPs. Limits: caching, since resolvers, operating systems, and applications cache answers for the TTL, and some ignore low TTLs, so failover takes minutes, not seconds, and not all clients switch; there's no awareness of actual load or connection state, so distribution is uneven when a few large resolvers represent many users; health checks only see what the DNS provider's checkers see; clients may pin to one IP with long-lived connections; and the response doesn't reflect capacity. So DNS is good for global traffic steering between regions, and failover with acceptable delay, but not for fine-grained load balancing. For that, use load balancers or anycast, which react in seconds. Common design is DNS to choose a region, and load balancers within the region, with low TTLs on failover records.`,
    f: ["Why is DNS failover slower than load balancer failover?","When would you use weighted DNS records?"]
  },
  56: {
    a: `Split-horizon DNS returns different answers for the same name depending on who is asking, typically internal versus external clients. For example, api.example.com resolves to a private IP for clients inside the VPC or corporate network, giving a direct path, and to a public load balancer address for clients on the internet. It's implemented with separate internal and external zones, such as a private hosted zone attached to a VPC alongside a public zone, or with views in BIND. You need it when internal traffic should stay on the private network for security, cost, or latency, when internal services shouldn't expose their private addresses publicly, to avoid hairpinning through NAT, and when the same hostname must work consistently for TLS certificates in both contexts. Pitfalls: the two views drift, so a record added to one but not the other causes it works from here but not there problems; clients using the wrong resolver, such as VPN users, containers, or public DNS configured on a laptop, see the wrong view; caching and search domains complicate debugging; and changes need to be made in both zones. To troubleshoot I query from each network location and compare, check which resolver answered, and manage both zones as code to keep them consistent. Document the design clearly for the team.`,
    f: ["How do you keep internal and external zones consistent?","What goes wrong for VPN users with split-horizon DNS?"]
  },
  57: {
    a: `DNS answers are cached at many layers, each with its own TTL behaviour, which is why both good and bad answers can stick around longer than expected. The layers: the application or runtime, such as a JVM which historically cached forever, and connection pools holding resolved addresses; the OS resolver cache, such as systemd-resolved or nscd; local forwarders and node-level caches like node-local DNS in Kubernetes; the recursive resolver at the ISP or cloud; and sometimes browsers. Negative caching stores the failure itself: an NXDOMAIN or SERVFAIL result is cached for the SOA minimum TTL or a configured value. So if a record is queried before it exists, the resolver remembers does not exist, and even after the record is created, clients keep getting the failure until the negative TTL expires. That's why a failure can be so persistent and inconsistent: different layers expire at different times, so some clients work and others don't. Diagnosis: query the authoritative server directly to confirm the record exists, query each resolver layer to find the stale one, and check TTLs in the answers. Remedies: flush the caches that hold the failure, wait out the TTL, set low negative TTLs, create records before anything queries them, and configure application DNS caching sensibly, for example setting JVM TTLs.`,
    f: ["Why do some clients work while others fail after a DNS fix?","How can a JVM's DNS caching cause an outage?"]
  },
  58: {
    a: `Selective DNS failure, where some names resolve and others don't, points to a specific zone, resolver, or record, not total DNS breakage, so I bisect. First compare a working and a failing name against the same resolver with dig, and read the status: NXDOMAIN means the record truly doesn't exist, SERVFAIL means the resolver couldn't get a valid answer, and a timeout means no response. Then compare resolvers: query the failing name against several resolvers and against the authoritative server directly. If the authoritative server answers but a resolver doesn't, the issue is in resolution: a stale negative cache, DNSSEC validation failing, or a forwarding rule. Use dig with trace to find a broken delegation, lame nameserver, or missing glue. Check whether the failing names share something: the same zone, domain, or suffix points to a delegation or forwarding rule, like a conditional forwarder for a private zone that doesn't exist anymore, or a search domain and ndots expansion producing wrong lookups. Check record types: an A works but AAAA fails, or large responses failing because UDP truncation and TCP fallback is blocked by a firewall. Also compare with the application's resolver path, using getent. Once located, fix the zone, forwarder, or firewall, and add monitoring on those names.`,
    f: ["What does SERVFAIL tell you compared with NXDOMAIN?","How can blocked TCP/53 cause selective failures?"]
  },
  59: {
    a: `DNSSEC cryptographically signs DNS records so resolvers can verify that answers weren't forged, protecting against cache poisoning and spoofing. Each zone signs its records, producing RRSIG signatures, and publishes its public key as a DNSKEY; a chain of trust is built because the parent zone holds a DS record, a hash of the child's key, up to the signed root. A validating resolver checks signatures along the chain. The cost is operational fragility. If something is wrong, such as expired signatures, a key rollover mistake, or a DS record at the registrar that doesn't match the zone's key, validating resolvers refuse the answer and return SERVFAIL for the whole domain, while non-validating resolvers still work. So the outage looks like DNS is broken for some users but not others, depending on which resolver they use, which is confusing. To diagnose: dig with the checking-disabled flag; if it returns a normal answer with checking disabled but SERVFAIL otherwise, validation is the cause. Tools like DNSViz or delv show where the chain breaks. Prevention: automate signing and key rollover, monitor signature expiry, carefully coordinate DS updates, test changes before publishing, and use managed DNS that handles it. DNSSEC protects integrity, not confidentiality.`,
    f: ["How does dig +cd help prove a DNSSEC failure?","Why does a DS record mismatch break resolution?"]
  },
  60: {
    a: `Classic DNS is plaintext over UDP port fifty-three, with TCP fallback for large responses, such as DNSSEC or zone transfers, and when UDP truncates. DNS over TLS wraps queries in a TLS session on port eight five three, and DNS over HTTPS carries them inside HTTPS on port four four three. Both add encryption and authentication of the resolver, protecting privacy and integrity from on-path observers. The operational impact: with plaintext DNS, network teams can see, log, filter, and monitor queries, and enforce policies such as blocking malicious domains. DoT is easy to identify and manage by port. DoH blends with normal HTTPS traffic, so it bypasses network-level DNS controls, and if a browser or application uses its own DoH resolver, internal names may not resolve, split-horizon DNS breaks, and security tools lose visibility. In enterprises, this means deciding on a policy: provide an internal encrypted resolver, configure clients to use it, and block or control external DoH endpoints. Performance considerations: encrypted transports add handshake overhead, mitigated by connection reuse. For troubleshooting, packet captures no longer show queries in clear text, so logging at the resolver is needed. Also TCP fallback must be allowed through firewalls, or large responses fail.`,
    f: ["Why does DoH complicate enterprise DNS controls?","When does DNS use TCP?"]
  },
  61: {
    a: `TCP opens a connection with a three-way handshake: SYN, SYN-ACK, ACK. It closes with a four-way exchange: each side sends a FIN and the other acknowledges, because each direction closes independently. The lingering states cause confusion. TIME_WAIT is held by the side that actively closes first, for around sixty seconds, so late packets from the old connection can't be mistaken for a new one and the last ACK can be retransmitted. Many TIME_WAIT sockets are normal on busy clients and proxies, but can exhaust ephemeral ports; the fix is connection reuse, a wider port range, and tcp_tw_reuse for outbound. CLOSE_WAIT is on the side that received a FIN: it's waiting for its own application to close the socket. If sockets pile up in CLOSE_WAIT, the application isn't closing connections after the peer hung up, which is a leak bug that eventually exhausts file descriptors. So TIME_WAIT is a kernel protocol mechanism, typically benign, while CLOSE_WAIT is application behaviour, typically a bug. I diagnose with ss counting states, and lsof to identify the process. In production, watch both counts as metrics. Also note that an abrupt close uses RST instead of FIN, which skips these states and indicates something different.`,
    f: ["Which side enters TIME_WAIT?","What causes CLOSE_WAIT to accumulate?"]
  },
  62: {
    a: `Every outbound connection consumes a local source port from the ephemeral range, and a connection is uniquely identified by the four-tuple of source address, source port, destination address, and destination port. To one destination address and port, the client has only the ephemeral range to work with, around twenty-eight thousand ports by default. Because closed connections linger in TIME_WAIT, a client creating many short connections to the same destination can run out, producing errors like cannot assign requested address, or connect timeouts and failures that appear under load. Diagnosis: check the port range, count sockets by state with ss, particularly TIME_WAIT to that destination, and see which process makes the connections. Fixes in order of preference: reuse connections through keep-alive and pooling; fix connection leaks; widen the ephemeral port range; enable tcp_tw_reuse for outbound connections; spread across more destination addresses or ports; and add source IP addresses. With NAT gateways, the gateway has its own limit of ports per destination, so adding more NAT addresses or spreading destinations helps; cloud NAT gateways expose metrics for port allocation errors. In Kubernetes, many pods behind one node address share the node's port space when SNAT is used. Monitor socket counts and NAT port errors to catch this before users do.`,
    f: ["Why does the limit apply per destination?","How can NAT gateways exhibit the same problem?"]
  },
  63: {
    a: `These are two different keep-alive mechanisms at different layers, and confusing them causes mysterious connection resets behind load balancers. TCP keep-alive is a kernel feature: after a period of idleness, it sends small probes to detect whether the peer is still there. The default idle time is about two hours, too long to be useful without tuning, but with tuning it can keep connections alive through NATs and firewalls that drop idle flows, and detect dead peers. HTTP keep-alive means reusing one TCP connection for multiple HTTP requests, avoiding repeated handshakes; the server decides how long it keeps idle connections open. Problems arise from timeout mismatches: a load balancer has an idle timeout, for example sixty seconds; if the backend's keep-alive timeout is shorter, the backend closes a connection just as the load balancer sends a request on it, producing 502 errors or resets. The rule: the server behind a load balancer should have an idle timeout longer than the load balancer's, so the load balancer is always the one to close first. The same applies to client pools holding connections longer than the server keeps them. Fixes: align timeouts across the chain, set TCP keep-alive probes below the shortest idle timeout in the path, and retry idempotent requests on reset.`,
    f: ["Why should the backend idle timeout exceed the load balancer's?","What does TCP keep-alive protect against?"]
  },
  64: {
    a: `Head-of-line blocking is when one stuck item delays everything queued behind it, and it occurs at different layers. In HTTP/1.1 it's at the application layer: one request at a time per connection, so a slow response blocks the next request on that connection. Browsers work around it by opening about six parallel connections per host, and developers used tricks like domain sharding. HTTP/2 fixes the application layer by multiplexing many streams over one TCP connection, so a slow response doesn't block others at the HTTP level. But it moves the problem down: TCP delivers bytes in order, so a single lost packet stalls every stream on that connection until it's retransmitted, which is TCP-level head-of-line blocking, painful on lossy mobile networks. HTTP/3 runs over QUIC, which is built on UDP and implements streams itself, so packet loss in one stream only blocks that stream, not the others. QUIC also integrates TLS 1.3 for faster connection setup, supports connection migration across networks using connection IDs, and avoids ossification of TCP. Operationally, HTTP/3 needs UDP to be allowed through firewalls and load balancers, and clients fall back to HTTP/2 when it's blocked. Benefits show up most on lossy or high-latency networks, while on clean networks the difference is small.`,
    f: ["Why does HTTP/2 still suffer head-of-line blocking?","What does QUIC do differently with streams?"]
  },
  65: {
    a: `Congestion control governs how fast TCP sends to avoid overwhelming the network. Loss-based algorithms such as Reno and CUBIC, long the default, ramp up the sending rate until they see packet loss, treat loss as the signal of congestion, then back off sharply, with slow start growing exponentially, then congestion avoidance growing more gently. The weakness: they only react after buffers overflow, which causes high queueing latency, bufferbloat, and they interpret random loss, as on wireless or long-distance paths, as congestion and throttle unnecessarily, hurting throughput. BBR, from Google, is model-based: it continuously estimates the path's bottleneck bandwidth and minimum round-trip time and paces sending to match the bandwidth-delay product, without filling queues. The result is higher throughput on lossy or long-haul links and lower latency under load. Trade-offs: BBR can be unfair to loss-based flows sharing a bottleneck, and early versions caused excess retransmissions, which BBRv2 addressed. Practically, I enable BBR on servers sending over wide-area or lossy paths, such as content delivery and cross-region transfers, along with the fq queueing discipline for pacing, and compare throughput and latency before and after. I inspect connection details with ss to see the algorithm, congestion window, and RTT.`,
    f: ["Why does loss-based congestion control struggle on lossy links?","What does BBR estimate to set its sending rate?"]
  },
  66: {
    a: `Inbound TCP connections pass through two kernel queues. The SYN queue holds incomplete, half-open connections that have received a SYN and sent SYN-ACK, waiting for the final ACK; its size is influenced by tcp_max_syn_backlog. The accept queue holds fully established connections waiting for the application to call accept; its size is the smaller of the application's listen backlog and somaxconn. A SYN flood sends many SYNs without completing handshakes, filling the SYN queue so legitimate clients are dropped. SYN cookies defend against it: when the queue is full, the server encodes the connection state cryptographically into the sequence number of the SYN-ACK, and doesn't store anything; only when a valid ACK comes back does it reconstruct the connection. So attackers can't exhaust memory, at the cost of losing some TCP options. Separately, the accept queue overflowing under load, because the application is too slow to accept or the backlog is too small, causes dropped connections and client timeouts or resets, visible in netstat statistics as listen queue overflows and in ss as the receive queue on listening sockets. Fixes: raise somaxconn and the application's backlog, make the application accept faster, scale out, and enable SYN cookies. Monitor the overflow counters.`,
    f: ["How do SYN cookies avoid storing state?","How do you see an accept queue overflow?"]
  },
  67: {
    a: `Every packet's next hop is chosen by consulting the routing table and picking the most specific matching route, known as longest-prefix match. The routing table lists destination prefixes with a next hop and interface. For example, a host might have a default route, zero-zero-zero-zero slash zero, via its gateway, a connected route for its own subnet, say ten dot zero dot zero dot zero slash twenty-four, directly on the interface, and a more specific route for another network via a VPN gateway. For a destination, the kernel finds all routes whose prefix contains the address and chooses the one with the longest prefix length, so a slash twenty-four beats a slash sixteen, which beats the default route. If multiple routes tie, metrics decide. I inspect with ip route and use ip route get with a destination to see exactly which route, interface, and source address would be used. The same logic applies in cloud route tables: the most specific route wins, with rules for ties between types. This explains many surprises: a more specific route from a peering or VPN silently overriding the internet path, overlapping CIDR ranges, blackhole routes after deleting a target, and asymmetric routing when return paths use different tables. When debugging connectivity, check routes on both ends and at every hop.`,
    f: ["Why does a /24 route beat a /16 route?","What does ip route get show?"]
  },
  68: {
    a: `CIDR notation, slash N, says how many bits are the network prefix, and the rest are host addresses. A slash twenty-four has two hundred fifty-six addresses, two hundred fifty-four usable; slash sixteen has sixty-five thousand five hundred thirty-six; slash twenty-eight has sixteen; usable hosts equal two to the power of thirty-two minus N, minus reserved addresses, and cloud providers reserve a few extra per subnet. Subnet planning is a day-zero decision that's painful to undo. For VPC design I choose a range large enough for growth, including Kubernetes pods, which can consume many addresses with VPC-native networking, and divide it by tier, public, private, and data, and by availability zone, leaving unallocated space for future subnets. Overlap is the main danger: VPCs or networks with overlapping CIDRs can't be peered or connected over VPN or transit gateway without NAT workarounds, and on-premises ranges must be considered too. So I allocate from a central IP address management plan, using the RFC 1918 private ranges, avoid commonly used defaults like 10.0.0.0/16 across all accounts, document allocations, and enforce through infrastructure as code and automated checks. Also plan for IPv6 and secondary ranges. A little extra address space is cheap compared with renumbering later.`,
    f: ["Why avoid default CIDR ranges across many VPCs?","How much address space should Kubernetes pods get?"]
  },
  69: {
    a: `Asymmetric routing is when a request and its reply take different network paths. For plain routers that's fine, but it breaks anything that tracks connection state. Suppose a request goes through firewall A, but the reply returns through firewall B. Firewall B never saw the SYN, so it considers the reply an invalid, unsolicited packet and drops it; firewall A never sees the reply, so its state entry times out. The result is connections that hang or fail, often intermittently depending on which path is chosen, which makes it hard to diagnose. The same affects stateful load balancers, NAT devices, and security appliances, and cloud environments with multiple interfaces, transit gateways, or ECMP paths. Typical causes: multiple default routes or multi-homed hosts, ECMP hashing differently in each direction, partial BGP advertisements, VPN plus direct paths, and policy routing mistakes. Diagnosis: packet captures on both sides and at the firewalls showing the SYN passing one device and the SYN-ACK another, traceroute from both ends, and routing table inspection. Fixes: make the paths symmetric by adjusting routing, use source-based policy routing so replies leave the same interface, use stateless filtering or state synchronisation between firewall pairs, or in clouds use appliance mode on transit gateways to pin flows. Design so that state is on the path.`,
    f: ["How would you prove the paths are asymmetric?","What is appliance mode on a transit gateway for?"]
  },
  70: {
    a: `BGP is the protocol that routes between autonomous systems, the backbone routing of the internet. Autonomous systems advertise which prefixes they can reach, and neighbours choose paths using policy and AS-path length. It's the glue between ISPs, clouds, and enterprises. The problem is that BGP is trust-based: it accepts advertisements from neighbours, and without filtering, a misconfiguration or malicious announcement spreads quickly. Outages come from route leaks, where an AS accidentally advertises routes it shouldn't, attracting traffic it can't handle; hijacks, where someone announces another's prefix, or a more specific one, which wins by longest prefix match, diverting or blackholing traffic; withdrawals, where a network accidentally withdraws its own routes, as in the large outage where a company's prefixes vanished and even its DNS became unreachable, hindering recovery; and session or configuration failures in cloud interconnects. Defences: prefix filters and maximum-prefix limits, RPKI route origin validation, IRR-based filtering, authentication on sessions, and monitoring of your prefixes with alerting on unexpected origin changes. Design for resilience: multiple providers, redundant BGP sessions with BFD, avoid circular dependencies where your recovery tools depend on the failed network, and keep out-of-band access. In interviews, show that you know both the mechanism and the blast radius.`,
    f: ["What does RPKI protect against?","Why do more specific prefixes win in a hijack?"]
  },
  71: {
    a: `These options connect networks, and they differ in topology, scale, bandwidth, and whether traffic crosses the public internet. VPC peering is a one-to-one private connection between two VPCs: simple and cheap, low latency, but not transitive, so A to B and B to C doesn't give A to C, and a mesh of peerings grows quadratically and becomes unmanageable. A transit gateway is a hub-and-spoke router connecting many VPCs and on-premises networks, with transitive routing and central route tables and segmentation, so it scales and simplifies, at a per-attachment and data processing cost. A site-to-site VPN uses IPsec tunnels over the public internet: quick to set up, encrypted, lower cost, but bandwidth per tunnel is limited and latency and jitter vary with the internet; BGP over multiple tunnels gives failover. Direct Connect, ExpressRoute, or Cloud Interconnect is a dedicated private link into the cloud: consistent low latency, higher bandwidth, and often lower data transfer costs, but needs lead time, a physical presence or partner, and redundancy design; it isn't encrypted by default, so add encryption if required. Choice: peering for a few VPCs, transit gateway for many, VPN for quick or backup connectivity, dedicated links for steady high-volume hybrid traffic, often with VPN as backup. Plan non-overlapping CIDRs.`,
    f: ["Why isn't VPC peering transitive an issue at scale?","Why combine Direct Connect with a VPN?"]
  },
  72: {
    a: `Normal routing decides by destination only. Policy-based routing, or source-based routing, lets me choose the route using other attributes such as the source address, firewall mark, incoming interface, or port, by using multiple routing tables and rules to select between them. On Linux: ip rule says traffic from a given source uses table one hundred, and that table has its own default route via a different gateway. I list with ip rule and view tables with ip route show table. When I need it: multi-homed hosts, with two interfaces on different networks, where replies must leave through the same interface the request arrived on, otherwise asymmetric routing and reverse-path filtering drop traffic; this is common with multiple ENIs on cloud instances, each in different subnets. Sending certain traffic, like backups or a specific tenant, over a particular link or through a VPN or inspection appliance; steering traffic by firewall mark, set by iptables, for fine control; and routing containers or namespaces differently. Pitfalls: complexity and hard-to-debug behaviour, because routing depends on rules, ordering, and priorities; rules and tables not persisting across reboots unless configured in the network manager; and interaction with rp_filter. Document it, test with ip route get including from the source address, and persist the configuration through the network configuration tooling.`,
    f: ["Why do multi-homed hosts need source-based routing?","How do you test which route a given source address would use?"]
  },
  73: {
    a: `Latency, packet loss, and jitter degrade connections differently, and conflating them sends debugging down the wrong path. Latency is the time for a packet to travel one way or round trip; high latency slows interactive requests and limits throughput per connection. Packet loss is the percentage of packets that never arrive; it triggers TCP retransmissions and congestion backoff, which reduces throughput sharply and adds tail latency, and breaks real-time media. Jitter is the variation in latency between packets; it harms voice, video, and any timing-sensitive traffic even if average latency is fine. mtr combines ping and traceroute and shows per-hop loss percentage, average, best, worst, and standard deviation of latency over many cycles. Reading it: real loss shows as loss that starts at a hop and continues to the final destination; loss at a single intermediate hop that disappears later is ICMP rate limiting, not a problem. A latency jump at a particular hop that persists to the end points to that link or region crossing; a high standard deviation indicates jitter. Use enough cycles for statistical value, and run in both directions. Then confirm with application-level measurements, since ICMP can be treated differently from real traffic, and use TCP-based probes if needed. The result guides who to contact and what to fix.`,
    f: ["How do you recognise real loss versus ICMP rate limiting in mtr?","Why does jitter matter even when average latency is fine?"]
  },
  74: {
    a: `Hairpinning, or NAT loopback, is when traffic from inside a network goes out to a public address and must turn around and come back to a host on the same network. Many setups don't support it. The scenario: an internal client resolves api.example.com to the public load balancer address; the packet goes to the gateway or load balancer, which would need to forward it back into the internal network to a backend. If the device doesn't support hairpin NAT, the connection fails or hangs; or with partial support, the backend replies directly to the client, bypassing the translating device, so the client sees a reply from an unexpected source address and drops it, a form of asymmetric routing. Symptoms: the service works from the internet but not from inside, or an internal service can't reach itself by its public name. Fixes: use split-horizon DNS so internal clients resolve the internal address directly; enable NAT loopback or hairpin support on the gateway, with source NAT on the hairpinned traffic so replies return via the device; in clouds, use internal load balancers or private endpoints; and in Kubernetes, enable hairpin mode on the bridge for pods reaching their own service. Prefer the DNS approach as it avoids unnecessary traffic and keeps flows simple.`,
    f: ["Why does the reply get dropped in a hairpin without SNAT?","How does split-horizon DNS remove the need for hairpinning?"]
  },
  75: {
    a: `An RST is TCP's abrupt way to say this connection is over now, as opposed to the graceful FIN. Applications see it as connection reset by peer, ECONNRESET, and in-flight data may be lost. Common causes: the peer sent it intentionally, for example connecting to a port with nothing listening gives an immediate RST, so the service is down or on a different port; the application crashed or was killed and the kernel reset open connections; a timeout mismatch where a load balancer or backend closed an idle connection and the client reused it, so the next request gets an RST; a firewall or middlebox injecting resets, or a stateful device that dropped its state after a NAT or conntrack timeout and now answers packets for that connection with RST; a listen queue overflow with reset behaviour configured; a protocol policy violation closing the connection; and abort-on-close with SO_LINGER set to zero. Diagnosis: packet capture to see who sent the RST and when, since the sender's address and TTL tell whether it's the real server or a device in between, then correlate with logs, deploys, and idle times. Fix the cause: align timeouts and keepalives, retry idempotent requests, and fix capacity or crashes.`,
    f: ["How do you tell whether a middlebox sent the RST?","Why do idle timeout mismatches produce resets?"]
  },
  76: {
    a: `A single HTTPS request touches DNS, TCP, TLS, and HTTP, and knowing each stage lets me attribute latency. First, DNS resolves the hostname to an IP, possibly from cache at several layers or through a full recursive walk. Second, TCP connects with the three-way handshake to port four four three, costing one round trip. Third, TLS: the client and server negotiate the version and cipher, the server proves its identity with a certificate, which the client validates, and they exchange keys; TLS 1.2 takes two round trips, 1.3 one, and resumption can reduce it further. Fourth, the client sends the HTTP request, possibly through a CDN, load balancer, or WAF, which may terminate TLS and forward to a backend, adding hops. Fifth, the server processes the request, perhaps calling databases and other services, and sends back the response; the time to first byte captures network latency plus server processing. Sixth, the client receives the response, may fetch more resources, and the browser parses and renders. Each stage can be measured: curl's timing variables break out DNS lookup, connect, TLS handshake, start transfer, and total, and browser developer tools show the same as a waterfall. Optimisations are connection reuse, HTTP/2 or HTTP/3, caching, CDNs, and TLS session resumption.`,
    f: ["How would you measure each stage with curl?","How many round trips happen before the first byte of an uncached HTTPS request?"]
  },
  77: {
    a: `TLS 1.3 streamlined the handshake for speed and removed legacy insecure options. In TLS 1.2 a full handshake takes two round trips before application data: the hello exchange, then the key exchange and finished messages. In TLS 1.3 the client sends its key share in the ClientHello, so the server can respond with its hello, certificate, and finished in one flight, and the connection is encrypted after a single round trip. TLS 1.3 also encrypts more of the handshake, including the certificate, removes weak ciphers, static RSA key exchange, compression, and renegotiation, and mandates forward secrecy. Session resumption allows zero round-trip data, 0-RTT: on a repeat connection, the client can send application data in its very first flight using a pre-shared key from an earlier session. The risk is replay: since the early data isn't protected by a fresh handshake from the server, an attacker can capture and resend that data, and the server may process it twice. So 0-RTT should be limited to idempotent requests, like GETs, or protected with anti-replay mechanisms such as single-use tickets, and sensitive actions shouldn't be allowed in early data; many deployments disable it or restrict it. Operationally, check that load balancers, proxies, and clients support TLS 1.3, and monitor for handshake failures when disabling old versions.`,
    f: ["Why is 0-RTT vulnerable to replay?","What did TLS 1.3 remove from TLS 1.2?"]
  },
  78: {
    a: `A client validates a server's certificate by building a chain of trust from the server's leaf certificate, signed by an intermediate CA, signed by a root CA that the client already trusts in its trust store. The client checks each signature, the validity dates, that the hostname matches the subject alternative names, key usage, and possibly revocation status. The missing intermediate trap: the server must send the leaf certificate plus the intermediates, but not necessarily the root. If an administrator installs only the leaf, some clients still succeed because browsers cache intermediates or fetch them using the authority information access extension, so it works in my browser. But programmatic clients such as curl, Java, Python, and mobile libraries don't fetch missing intermediates and fail with errors like unable to get local issuer certificate. Diagnosis: run openssl s_client with the server name and look at the chain the server actually sends; online SSL checkers flag incomplete chains. Fix: configure the server or load balancer with the full chain bundle, the leaf followed by intermediates, in the right order. Other causes of validation failure: expired certificates, hostname mismatch, private CAs missing from the trust store, and clock skew. Automate renewal and test with a non-browser client.`,
    f: ["Why does an incomplete chain work in some browsers?","How do you check the chain a server actually sends?"]
  },
  79: {
    a: `These 5xx errors usually come from a gateway, proxy, or load balancer, and each points to a distinct backend condition. A 502 Bad Gateway means the proxy received an invalid response or couldn't get one: the backend crashed mid-response, closed the connection, reset it, or spoke the wrong protocol; common causes are application crashes, keep-alive timeout mismatches where the backend closes idle connections first, or a TLS mismatch to the backend. A 503 Service Unavailable means there is no healthy backend to send the request to, or the service is deliberately shedding load: all targets failing health checks, a deployment with no ready pods, overload, or a circuit breaker open. A 504 Gateway Timeout means the backend didn't respond within the proxy's timeout, so the backend is slow or hung: a slow query, a dependency stalled, thread exhaustion, or the proxy timeout being shorter than legitimate processing time. To debug, I check which component emitted the error through response headers and access logs; look at backend health status and target counts; read backend logs and crashes around the time; check resource saturation and latency; and compare proxy timeouts with backend behaviour. Fixes: stabilise the app, align keepalive and timeouts, fix health checks, add capacity, and set sensible retries. Alert on each separately.`,
    f: ["Which of the three usually indicates no healthy targets?","How do keep-alive mismatches cause 502s?"]
  },
  80: {
    a: `Idempotency means applying the same request multiple times has the same effect as applying it once, and it's the foundation of safe retries in distributed systems. Safe methods have no side effects: GET, HEAD, and OPTIONS. Idempotent methods include GET, PUT, and DELETE: repeating a PUT sets the same value, repeating a DELETE leaves the resource deleted. POST is not idempotent by default, as each call may create another resource or charge a card again. Retries happen constantly, due to timeouts, network failures, and client or proxy logic, and a timeout doesn't tell the client whether the server processed the request, so retrying a non-idempotent operation risks duplicates, like double charges. Idempotency keys solve this: the client generates a unique key per logical operation and sends it in a header; the server records the key with the result the first time, and on any repeat with the same key returns the stored result instead of executing again. Implementation details: store keys with the response, with an expiry; make the check-and-set atomic to handle concurrent duplicates; scope keys per client; and validate that a reused key comes with the same payload. Combine with retries using exponential backoff and jitter. Make message consumers idempotent too, since at-least-once delivery means duplicates.`,
    f: ["Why is POST not idempotent by default?","How must the server store an idempotency key to be safe under concurrency?"]
  },
  81: {
    a: `These browser mechanisms govern credentials and cross-origin behaviour, and they're a frequent source of works locally, breaks in production. Cookie attributes: Secure means the cookie is sent only over HTTPS; HttpOnly makes it unreadable by JavaScript, mitigating token theft via XSS; SameSite controls whether it is sent on cross-site requests: Strict never, Lax only on top-level navigations such as clicking a link, and None always, which requires Secure. SameSite mitigates cross-site request forgery. Domain and path scope where it's sent, and expiry controls lifetime. CORS, cross-origin resource sharing, addresses a different problem: the same-origin policy prevents a page from one origin reading responses from another. CORS lets a server opt in via headers such as Access-Control-Allow-Origin, allowed methods and headers, and whether credentials are allowed. For non-simple requests, the browser sends a preflight OPTIONS request first, and the server must answer correctly. Typical production bugs: a frontend on a different domain from the API failing with CORS errors because the allowed origin is wrong or the preflight isn't handled by the load balancer or gateway; credentials mode requiring a specific origin instead of a wildcard; cookies not sent because of SameSite or missing Secure; and cookies set on the wrong domain. CORS is enforced by browsers, not servers, so it isn't an authentication mechanism.`,
    f: ["Why does a wildcard origin not work with credentials?","What is a CORS preflight request?"]
  },
  82: {
    a: `HTTP caching avoids refetching unchanged content, using freshness directives and validators. Freshness is set by Cache-Control: max-age says the response is fresh for that many seconds, and can be served from cache without contacting the origin. no-cache is often misunderstood: it means the cache must revalidate with the origin before using the stored copy, not that it can't be stored; no-store means don't store at all, used for sensitive data. public and private say whether shared caches such as CDNs may store it, and s-maxage sets a shared-cache lifetime. Validators let a cache check whether a stale copy is still valid cheaply: the server sends an ETag, a version identifier, or Last-Modified; the client later sends If-None-Match or If-Modified-Since; and if unchanged, the server answers 304 Not Modified with no body, saving bandwidth. Misconfiguration causes problems: no caching headers leads to heuristic caching or none; long max-age on files that change causes stale content, so use content-hashed filenames with a very long lifetime for assets and short or revalidated caching for HTML entry points; missing Vary headers can serve the wrong variant, such as compressed content to a client that can't handle it; and caching personalised responses publicly is a security risk. I verify headers with curl and test the revalidation flow.`,
    f: ["What is the difference between no-cache and no-store?","How does an ETag validation save bandwidth?"]
  },
  83: {
    a: `In standard TLS only the server proves its identity, with a certificate the client verifies. Mutual TLS adds client authentication: both sides present certificates and verify each other, establishing strong two-way identity and encrypted transport. It replaces or supplements passwords and API tokens with cryptographic identity. Where it's used in production: service-to-service communication inside microservice architectures, commonly via a service mesh that issues short-lived certificates to workloads and rotates them automatically, giving zero-trust internal networking; securing APIs between business partners or from devices, where each client has its own certificate; connections to databases, message brokers, and Kubernetes components, which use mTLS internally between the API server, kubelets, and etcd; and as the basis of identity-aware access. Benefits: strong authentication tied to workload identity, protection against impersonation and eavesdropping, and a good fit for authorisation policies based on identity. Costs: certificate lifecycle management is the hard part, covering issuance, rotation, revocation, and CA protection; debugging is harder because traffic is encrypted and failures show as handshake errors; clock skew and expired certificates cause outages; and clients must be able to hold and present certificates. Mitigation is automation with cert-manager, SPIFFE or mesh CAs, short lifetimes, and monitoring of expiry. Roll out gradually, using permissive mode first.`,
    f: ["Why are short-lived certificates preferred for mTLS?","What breaks first when mTLS certificates expire?"]
  },
  84: {
    a: `A web application firewall inspects HTTP and HTTPS requests at layer seven to block application-layer attacks, but it's a filter, not a substitute for secure code. What it blocks: common injection patterns such as SQL injection, cross-site scripting, and command injection from the OWASP Top Ten; known bad bots and scanners; suspicious request shapes; and abusive request rates, with rules, managed rule sets, and custom rules. It also supports virtual patching, blocking exploit patterns for a known vulnerability while the real fix is prepared. Limits: it can be bypassed by obfuscation and novel attacks, it produces false positives that block legitimate users unless tuned, so deploy in detect mode first and then enforce; it can't understand business logic flaws like broken authorisation; it sees only what is decrypted at its position, so it must sit after TLS termination; and it adds latency. Relation to layer-seven DDoS protection: floods of seemingly legitimate requests are handled by rate limiting, bot management, and challenges, features often delivered in the same product, but the WAF isn't designed to absorb volumetric network floods, which need upstream scrubbing and anycast capacity. Best practice: layer it with secure coding, input validation, patching, and monitoring, and review its logs regularly.`,
    f: ["Why deploy a WAF in detect mode first?","What is virtual patching?"]
  },
  85: {
    a: `DDoS attacks target different layers, so the defences differ. Layer three and four attacks are volumetric or protocol-based: UDP and ICMP floods, amplification through DNS or NTP reflection, and SYN floods. The goal is to saturate bandwidth or exhaust connection state. Defences: upstream scrubbing and anycast capacity at the provider or CDN, since no single origin can absorb a large flood; SYN cookies; rate limiting and filtering of spoofed or unneeded protocols; and hiding the origin, so attackers can't bypass the edge. Layer seven attacks exhaust application resources with seemingly valid requests: HTTP floods, slow-connection attacks like slowloris, and expensive-endpoint abuse such as search or login. The goal is to exhaust CPU, threads, or the database. Defences: a WAF with rate limiting per client, bot detection and challenges, caching so floods hit the cache, timeouts and connection limits, protecting expensive endpoints with authentication or queues, and autoscaling with cost caps. Across both: baseline normal traffic to detect anomalies, have a runbook and contacts at the provider, design for graceful degradation, and test with game days. A layered approach is needed because a defence at one layer is blind to the other. Network scrubbing doesn't see application semantics, and a WAF can't stop a bandwidth flood.`,
    f: ["Why can't a WAF stop a volumetric flood?","What makes slowloris effective?"]
  },
  86: {
    a: `Cloud network security is layered, because no single control suffices; the controls stack from the edge down to the workload. At the edge: a CDN with DDoS protection and a WAF to absorb, scrub, and filter at the perimeter. At the network level: VPC segmentation into public, private, and data subnets, with network ACLs as a coarse subnet-level guard, and route tables that enforce what can reach where. At the instance level: security groups as stateful, least-privilege firewalls, referencing other groups for tiered access. For internal traffic: Kubernetes network policies or service mesh authorisation for micro-segmentation, default-deny between services, and mTLS for encryption and identity. For egress: NAT gateways with filtering, proxies or firewalls with domain allow-lists, and private endpoints for cloud services so traffic doesn't traverse the internet. For access: no public SSH, using session managers or identity-aware proxies, and VPN or zero-trust access for admins. For visibility: flow logs, DNS logs, WAF logs, and intrusion detection, centralised and monitored. For data: encryption in transit and at rest. For governance: infrastructure as code with policy checks, and guardrails that prevent public exposure. Each layer assumes the others may fail, so a breach in one does not become full compromise.`,
    f: ["Why default-deny between services?","How do private endpoints reduce exposure?"]
  },
  87: {
    a: `The perimeter model, castle and moat, builds a hard outer wall with a firewall and VPN, and trusts everything inside. Its problem is that one breach, a phished laptop or a compromised workload, gives broad implicit access, and attackers move laterally across the flat internal network. Cloud, remote work, and SaaS also dissolved the perimeter: users and workloads are everywhere, so there is no single inside. Zero trust assumes the network is hostile and verifies every request regardless of location. The principles: authenticate and authorise every request using strong identity of users and workloads, with MFA and device health; grant least privilege, scoped narrowly and ideally just in time; encrypt all traffic, including internal; segment the network so a compromise stays contained; and continuously monitor and log decisions, evaluating context such as device posture, location, and behaviour. In practice: identity-aware proxies replace broad VPN access for applications; service meshes provide mTLS and authorisation policies between services; network policies default-deny; and short-lived credentials replace static keys. It's a strategy, not a product, and adoption is incremental. Trade-offs: more operational complexity, certificate and identity management overhead, and the need for good policy definition and observability. It complements, rather than replaces, basic network hygiene.`,
    f: ["Why does a flat internal network enable lateral movement?","What replaces broad VPN access in a zero-trust design?"]
  },
  88: {
    a: `All three sit between clients and servers, but they face different directions and serve different roles. A forward proxy sits in front of clients and proxies their outbound requests: corporate egress proxies, content filtering, caching, auditing, and anonymity. A reverse proxy sits in front of servers and fronts inbound traffic: TLS termination, load balancing, caching, compression, routing by host or path, and hiding backend topology; nginx, HAProxy, and Envoy are common, and Kubernetes ingress controllers are reverse proxies. An API gateway is a specialised reverse proxy focused on API management: authentication and authorisation, rate limiting and quotas, request and response transformation, routing to microservices, API versioning, usage analytics, and developer portal features. Concrete uses: a forward proxy to restrict which sites servers can reach and log outbound calls; a reverse proxy to expose many internal services through one TLS endpoint and balance load; an API gateway to publish a public API with keys, quotas, and consistent security. The overlap is real, since many products do several jobs, so the distinction is about the role. Considerations: all are potential single points of failure and performance bottlenecks, need redundancy, and add a hop to troubleshoot, requiring client IP preservation via headers. Choose the lightest component that meets the need.`,
    f: ["How does an API gateway differ from a plain reverse proxy?","Why might servers need a forward proxy?"]
  },
  89: {
    a: `Exposing an internal service safely is about minimising the attack surface and adding layers between the internet and the workload. The typical path is: internet to a CDN or edge with DDoS protection and a WAF, then a public load balancer as the single managed entry point terminating TLS, then the backend in a private subnet with no public IP, reachable only from the load balancer's security group. Further controls: authenticate at the edge or gateway, with OAuth, API keys, or mTLS depending on the clients; apply rate limiting and request size limits; expose only the specific paths and methods needed, not the whole service; use TLS with modern versions and managed certificates; restrict the origin so it accepts traffic only from the CDN or load balancer; harden the service itself, with patching, least-privilege identities, and input validation; and separate the exposed service from sensitive data stores with network segmentation. Observability matters: access logs, WAF logs, and alerts on anomalies. Consider alternatives that avoid public exposure entirely: private connectivity, VPN, private endpoints, or an identity-aware proxy for internal users and partners. Before going live, run security review and testing, such as a penetration test, and prepare incident response. Keep the exposure under change control and review it periodically.`,
    f: ["Why should the backend have no public IP?","How do you make sure traffic only reaches the origin via the CDN?"]
  },
  90: {
    a: `gRPC uses HTTP/2 with binary Protobuf messages for efficient, strongly typed RPC, supports streaming in both directions, generates client and server code from contracts, and has low overhead, which makes it great for internal service-to-service calls. REST is typically JSON over HTTP/1.1 or HTTP/2: human-readable, widely supported by browsers and tools, easy to debug and cache, but larger and without a strict contract unless OpenAPI is used. The networking catch with gRPC is load balancing. gRPC clients keep one long-lived HTTP/2 connection and multiplex many requests over it, so a layer-four load balancer balances connections, not requests: all of a client's requests go to one backend, and when new backends are added or after a scale-out, existing connections don't rebalance, leaving hot and idle instances. Solutions: use a layer-seven load balancer or proxy that understands HTTP/2 and balances per request, such as Envoy, or an ingress that supports gRPC; use client-side load balancing with service discovery; use a service mesh; or set maximum connection age on servers so connections recycle and rebalance. Other issues: browsers can't speak native gRPC without gRPC-Web, health checking needs the gRPC health protocol, deadlines and retries need explicit configuration, and debugging binary traffic requires tooling such as grpcurl.`,
    f: ["Why does an L4 balancer distribute gRPC unevenly?","What does max connection age do?"]
  },
  91: {
    a: `The layer a load balancer operates at determines what it can route on, and what it costs. A layer-four balancer works on IP and port for TCP or UDP, without parsing the payload. It balances per connection, is very fast with low overhead and high throughput, can preserve the client's source IP, handles any protocol including databases and TLS passthrough, and is simple to scale. What it can't see: URLs, headers, cookies, or HTTP status codes, so it cannot route by path, do canary by header, or give request-level metrics. A layer-seven balancer terminates and parses the protocol, typically HTTP: it can route by host, path, header, or cookie, rewrite requests, terminate TLS, authenticate, apply WAF rules, retry on failure, split traffic by weight, and provide detailed request metrics, access logs, and per-request balancing, important for HTTP/2 and gRPC. The costs: more CPU and latency per request, the client IP is carried in headers, and it only understands the protocols it supports. Layer seven sees decrypted content only if it terminates TLS, which raises the question of where certificates live. I choose layer seven for web and API traffic needing content-based routing and observability, layer four for non-HTTP protocols, extreme throughput, static IPs, or end-to-end encryption.`,
    f: ["What can an L7 balancer do that an L4 cannot?","Why might you terminate TLS at the load balancer?"]
  },
  92: {
    a: `The algorithm decides which backend receives each request, and the right choice depends on request uniformity and whether I need stickiness. Round robin rotates evenly and works well when requests and backends are uniform. Weighted round robin gives larger backends a larger share, and supports canary splits. Least connections sends to the backend with the fewest active connections, better when request cost varies or connections are long-lived, such as streaming or uploads. Least response time or power of two choices picks between two random backends the less loaded one, which avoids herding and works well in large fleets. IP hash maps a client to a backend, giving rough affinity without cookies, but unevenness with NATed clients. Consistent hashing on a key, such as a user ID or cache key, sends the same key to the same backend and minimises remapping when backends are added or removed, ideal for caches and sharded stateful services, though it can create hot spots unless virtual nodes or bounded loads are used. Random is simple and surprisingly good at scale. Whatever the algorithm, health checks, slow start for new instances, and connection draining matter as much. I verify the choice by measuring per-backend load, latency, and error rates, and adjust when the distribution is skewed.`,
    f: ["Why is least connections better for variable request cost?","Why does consistent hashing suit caches?"]
  },
  93: {
    a: `Load balancers route only to backends that pass health checks, so the check's design is safety-critical: a bad one can take down a healthy fleet. Active checks have the balancer probe an endpoint on each backend periodically; passive checks observe real traffic and mark a backend unhealthy after consecutive errors. Key settings are the interval, timeout, and the healthy and unhealthy thresholds. The classic cascade is a deep health check that tests dependencies like the database: when the database slows, every backend fails its check, the balancer removes all of them, and a partial degradation becomes a total outage. Other mistakes: a timeout shorter than normal latency under load, so overloaded but working instances are ejected and the rest get more load and fail too; thresholds so aggressive that transient blips eject healthy instances; and a shallow check that only tests the port, so broken instances stay in rotation. Best practice: separate liveness from readiness; keep the check cheap and local; handle dependency failure with graceful degradation instead of ejecting everything; use a panic mode or minimum healthy percentage, so the balancer keeps routing if too many targets fail; and test failure scenarios deliberately.`,
    f: ["Why is a deep health check that tests the database dangerous?","What is panic mode in a load balancer?"]
  },
  94: {
    a: `Session affinity pins a client to the same backend across requests, using a cookie that names the backend or a hash of the source IP. It's sometimes necessary, but undermines what load balancing offers, which is why it's called an anti-pattern. The problems: uneven load, since heavy users or many clients behind one NAT address land on one backend; fragile failover, because when the pinned backend dies, those users lose their session state; hard deployments and scaling, since draining a backend means waiting for sessions to end and new instances get no traffic from existing users; and it hides state in server memory, a design smell. The better approach is to make the application stateless: keep session data in a shared store such as Redis or a database, or in signed tokens like JWTs, so any backend can serve any request. Then load balancing is even, instances are disposable, autoscaling works properly, and failures are invisible to users. When affinity is legitimately needed, for example WebSockets, caches with local state, or legacy applications, I use cookie-based affinity with a bounded lifetime instead of IP hash, plan for backend loss, keep sessions recoverable, and monitor per-backend load. Document it as technical debt with a plan to remove it.`,
    f: ["What alternatives remove the need for sticky sessions?","Why is IP-hash affinity unreliable?"]
  },
  95: {
    a: `WebSockets are long-lived, stateful, full-duplex connections, and they break assumptions load balancers make about short request and response cycles. The challenges: load is decided at connect time and never rebalances while connections persist for minutes or hours, so scale-out doesn't help existing connections; a backend restart or deploy drops all its sockets, causing reconnect storms; idle timeouts on balancers and proxies close quiet connections; and the upgrade handshake must be supported end to end. Solutions: use a balancer that supports the WebSocket upgrade and configure generous idle timeouts, with ping and pong heartbeats from the application to keep connections active and detect dead peers; use least connections instead of round robin so new connections go to the least loaded instance; implement client reconnection with exponential backoff and jitter to avoid thundering herds; drain gracefully during deploys by stopping new connections, signalling clients to reconnect elsewhere, and giving a termination grace period; keep connection state out of the process where possible, by using a shared pub/sub backplane such as Redis so a message can reach a client whichever backend holds its socket; and size capacity by concurrent connections and memory per connection, including file descriptor limits. Monitor connection counts per instance, and consider rebalancing by periodically closing old connections.`,
    f: ["Why do reconnect storms happen after a deploy?","How does a pub/sub backplane help WebSocket scaling?"]
  },
  96: {
    a: `Connection draining, or graceful deregistration, lets a backend finish its in-flight requests before it's removed from the pool, so deploys and scale-in don't drop requests. Without it, deregistering a backend instantly cuts off requests mid-response, and users see errors or resets on every deploy. With draining, the load balancer stops sending new requests to the backend but keeps existing connections open for a configured timeout, so in-flight work completes, and then removes it. For zero-downtime, it must work with the application: on SIGTERM the application stops accepting new connections, finishes in-flight requests, and exits within the grace period; readiness should flip to not-ready first so traffic stops; and the timeouts must be consistent, the load balancer's deregistration delay should be at least as long as the longest typical request, and the orchestrator's termination grace period longer than that. In Kubernetes, there's a race between endpoint removal and pod termination, so a preStop sleep gives time for the change to propagate. For long-lived connections like WebSockets, draining takes longer, so signal clients to reconnect. Also watch for keep-alive connections that the client keeps reusing: send connection close headers during drain. Test by deploying under load and watching for errors. Set realistic timeouts, since too long slows deployments.`,
    f: ["Why does Kubernetes need a preStop delay?","What must the deregistration delay be relative to request duration?"]
  },
  97: {
    a: `A VPC is your isolated virtual network in the cloud, with its own address range, such as 10.0.0.0/16. Subnets are slices of that range, each tied to one availability zone; whether a subnet is public or private is decided by routing, not by a property. A route table says where traffic goes: the main components are the local route for VPC-internal traffic and a default route. An internet gateway provides two-way connectivity between the VPC and the internet; a public subnet has a route to it and instances with public addresses. A NAT gateway lives in a public subnet and gives private subnets outbound-only internet access, translating their private addresses to its own public address, without allowing unsolicited inbound connections. A typical layout has public subnets in each AZ for load balancers and NAT gateways, private subnets for application servers, and isolated data subnets with no internet route for databases. Security groups and network ACLs sit on top. Design points: one NAT gateway per AZ for resilience and to avoid cross-AZ charges; endpoints for cloud services so traffic doesn't traverse NAT; non-overlapping CIDRs for peering; and flow logs for visibility. Understanding the routing table explains most of why something can or can't reach the internet.`,
    f: ["What makes a subnet public rather than private?","Why use one NAT gateway per AZ?"]
  },
  98: {
    a: `VPC endpoints let resources reach cloud services, and partner or your own services, privately, without traversing the public internet or a NAT gateway. In AWS there are two types. Gateway endpoints, for S3 and DynamoDB, are a route-table entry pointing to the service; they have no hourly cost and keep traffic on the provider's network. Interface endpoints, PrivateLink, create an elastic network interface with a private IP in my subnet that fronts the service, so I reach it via a private address and private DNS name; they cost per hour and per gigabyte but work for hundreds of services and for my own services exposed to other VPCs or accounts without peering or overlapping CIDR concerns. Why use them: security, since traffic never leaves the private network and the VPC can have no internet route at all; endpoint policies restrict which resources or principals can be accessed, helping prevent data exfiltration; reduced NAT gateway data processing charges, which can be large for heavy S3 traffic; potentially lower latency and better reliability; and compliance requirements for private connectivity. Considerations: DNS settings must resolve the service name to the endpoint, endpoints are regional and per VPC, interface endpoints need security groups, and cross-region access needs more design.`,
    f: ["How does a gateway endpoint differ from an interface endpoint?","How do endpoint policies reduce data exfiltration risk?"]
  },
  99: {
    a: `Load balancing, autoscaling, and health checks form the elasticity loop. A metric such as CPU, request rate, or latency crosses a threshold, and the autoscaler adds instances. A new instance boots, starts the application, and registers with the load balancer; the health check passes, and then it begins receiving traffic. The balancer distributes load across healthy members, and health checks remove failing ones. The warm-up problem is that new instances are not immediately useful: they need boot time, image pull, application start, cache warm-up, JIT compilation, and connection pool filling. If the load balancer sends full traffic as soon as the health check passes, a cold instance can be overwhelmed, fail health checks, get removed, and cause thrashing. Mitigations: readiness checks that reflect true readiness; slow start settings that ramp traffic gradually to new targets; instance warm-up periods in the autoscaling policy so metrics from warming instances aren't counted; warm pools or pre-initialised capacity; faster images and startup; scaling on leading indicators like queue depth or request rate, with enough headroom; scheduled or predictive scaling for known peaks; and cooldowns to avoid flapping. Also protect backends with concurrency limits and load shedding. Test by simulating a surge and measuring time to effective capacity.`,
    f: ["What does slow start do for new targets?","Why shouldn't warming instances count in scaling metrics?"]
  },
  100: {
    a: `Anycast advertises the same IP address from many locations, and the network, through BGP, routes each client's packets to the topologically nearest location. That gives low latency without client-side logic, and automatic failover, because if one location stops advertising, traffic shifts to the next nearest. It's the backbone of global CDNs and DNS: the root and public DNS resolvers use anycast, and CDN edges and global load balancers advertise one IP from hundreds of points of presence. Benefits: proximity, resilience, and DDoS resistance, since attack traffic is distributed across many locations instead of concentrating on one, so each absorbs a fraction. Caveats: routing follows BGP policy, not true latency, so the nearest by network path isn't always the fastest; flows can shift mid-connection when routes change, which is a problem for long-lived TCP connections unless the design copes, for example by short-lived requests or state at the edge; troubleshooting is harder because the same IP leads to different machines depending on where you are, so I need to identify which site answered; and capacity must be planned per site. Cloud global load balancers use anycast front ends, then route internally over the provider's backbone to the best healthy region. For unicast alternatives, GeoDNS achieves similar steering with DNS caching limits.`,
    f: ["Why does anycast help against DDoS?","What problems can anycast cause for long-lived TCP connections?"]
  },
  101: {
    a: `Multi-region architecture serves users from the nearest region and survives a regional failure, but introduces cross-region latency and data consistency challenges. For traffic steering, I use GeoDNS or latency-based routing, or an anycast global load balancer, with health checks per region so traffic shifts away from a failed region; low TTLs on failover records. For the application tier, run the stateless services in each region, deployed through the same pipeline, with capacity planned so a surviving region can absorb the failed region's load. The data layer is the hard part: choose a model, active-passive with asynchronous replication and promotion on failure, simpler but with a recovery time and some data loss; or active-active with multi-region databases, which needs conflict handling, regional write ownership, or strong-consistency systems that trade latency. Define RPO and RTO per dataset. Networking: private, encrypted connectivity between regions through the provider backbone, peering or transit gateways, non-overlapping CIDRs, and control over cross-region data transfer costs. Operations: failover runbooks that are tested through regular game days, since untested failover usually fails; configuration and secrets replicated; observability that spans regions; and avoiding hidden single-region dependencies, such as a control plane, a global service, or a database in one region. Weigh the cost and complexity against the availability requirement.`,
    f: ["What is the hard part of multi-region design?","Why must a surviving region have spare capacity?"]
  },
  102: {
    a: `The shared responsibility model splits cloud security and networking duties: the provider secures the infrastructure, and the customer secures what they build on it. The provider is responsible for security of the cloud: the physical network and data centres, hardware, the hypervisor, the backbone between regions and zones, and the isolation primitives of the virtual network. The customer is responsible for security in the cloud: designing the VPC and subnets, routes, security groups and network ACLs, which ports and sources are open, exposure of services to the internet, TLS configuration and certificate management, firewall and WAF rules, identity and access policies, patching guest operating systems and applications, encryption choices and key management, logging and monitoring, and the architecture's resilience across zones and regions. The line shifts with the service model: with infrastructure as a service the customer handles more, with managed services the provider handles patching of the underlying platform, but configuration, access, and data protection stay with the customer. Typical failures are on the customer side: an open security group, a public bucket, overly broad IAM, or unmonitored logs. In practice I treat the provider's guarantees as a foundation and use guardrails, policy as code, configuration scanning, and flow logs to keep my side correct.`,
    f: ["Which side is responsible for an open security group?","How does the split change for managed services?"]
  },
  103: {
    a: `In dynamic environments with autoscaling and containers, instances come and go with changing IP addresses, so clients can't hardcode them. Service discovery provides current, healthy endpoints. Patterns: client-side discovery, where the client queries a registry such as Consul or Eureka and picks an instance itself, which gives control but puts logic in every client; and server-side discovery, where the client calls a stable endpoint, a load balancer or proxy, that consults the registry, which keeps clients simple. DNS plays a central role as the universal interface. In Kubernetes, every Service gets a DNS name, such as service dot namespace dot svc, resolved by CoreDNS to a stable cluster IP, or for headless services to the individual pod IPs, and the control plane keeps the endpoints updated with readiness. Cloud service discovery and Consul also expose DNS interfaces, with SRV records giving ports. Caveats of DNS-based discovery: caching and TTLs mean clients may use stale addresses, so TTLs must be low and clients must re-resolve; some runtimes cache forever; DNS returns no health or load information beyond what the registry filters; and large record sets or UDP truncation can be an issue. A service mesh adds richer discovery through its control plane. I choose based on scale and needs, and ensure health information drives the registry.`,
    f: ["How do headless services differ from regular ones in Kubernetes?","Why can DNS caching break service discovery?"]
  },
  104: {
    a: `Data transfer is a major and often surprising cloud cost, and network design can reduce it. The cost hierarchy, roughly from cheapest to most expensive: traffic within the same availability zone over private IPs is usually free or cheapest; cross-AZ traffic within a region carries a per-gigabyte charge in both directions, often overlooked; cross-region traffic costs more; and internet egress is the most expensive. NAT gateways add a data processing charge on top. Tactics: keep chatty services in the same AZ where resilience allows, using topology-aware routing in Kubernetes to prefer same-zone endpoints; use gateway and interface endpoints so traffic to S3 and other services bypasses NAT and the internet; put a CDN in front of content, since CDN egress is typically cheaper than origin egress and offloads the origin; compress responses and use efficient protocols and formats; cache aggressively; process data where it lives instead of moving it across regions; use private connectivity such as Direct Connect for heavy hybrid transfer, which has lower per-gigabyte rates; and replicate across regions only what's needed. Visibility comes first: use cost allocation tags, flow logs, and billing analysis to find the top talkers, then optimise those. Weigh savings against resilience, as removing cross-AZ traffic can reduce availability.`,
    f: ["Why is cross-AZ traffic often overlooked?","How do VPC endpoints reduce NAT costs?"]
  },
  105: {
    a: `Rate limiting protects services from overload and abuse. The algorithm shapes how bursts are handled. A token bucket refills tokens at a steady rate, and each request spends one; it allows bursts up to the bucket size, then throttles to the refill rate, which suits APIs where short bursts are acceptable. A leaky bucket processes requests at a fixed rate, queueing or dropping the excess, which smooths traffic into a steady output and suits protecting a downstream system that can't take bursts. Other approaches are fixed windows, simple but allowing double bursts at window boundaries, sliding windows, more accurate, and concurrency limits. Where to enforce: at the edge, CDN or WAF, to stop abuse before it consumes bandwidth; at the API gateway or load balancer for per-client and per-route quotas, keyed by API key, user, or IP; and in the application or service for fine-grained limits and protection of expensive operations, plus client-side limits and backoff. In distributed systems, counters need shared state, such as Redis, or approximate local limits. Good practice: return 429 with a Retry-After header and rate limit headers; choose keys carefully, since IP-based limits hurt users behind NAT; set different limits per tier and endpoint; combine with load shedding and circuit breakers; and monitor the rejection rates.`,
    f: ["How does a token bucket differ from a leaky bucket?","Why return a Retry-After header?"]
  },
  106: {
    a: `Can't reach the service is ambiguous, so I work up the stack methodically instead of guessing, and each layer has a definitive test. First DNS: does the name resolve, and to the right IP, with dig or getent. Second layer three: is the host reachable, with ping if ICMP is allowed, and which route does traffic take, with ip route get and traceroute. Third the port: can I open a TCP connection, with nc or curl, which separates refused from timeout; refused means the host is reachable but nothing is listening, timeout means packets are dropped somewhere. Fourth, on the server, is the process listening on the expected address and port, with ss, and not just on localhost. Fifth the firewalls: host rules, security groups, network ACLs, and any intermediate firewall, using flow logs to see rejects. Sixth TLS: does the handshake and certificate verify, with openssl s_client. Seventh the application: does it respond correctly, what do its logs say, is it overloaded or failing health checks, and what does the load balancer show about target health. I compare with a working client or location to find what differs, and capture packets if layers are ambiguous. Documenting the layer where it fails tells me which team owns the fix.`,
    f: ["What separates refused from timeout?","Where do flow logs help in this process?"]
  },
  107: {
    a: `tcpdump captures packets to show what is actually on the wire, ground truth when logs and assumptions disagree. The skill is targeted capture and reading the result. I start by deciding the question: does traffic arrive, does the handshake complete, are there resets or retransmissions, does DNS get answered? Then capture on the right interface with a precise filter, a host and port, or TCP flags such as SYN and RST, with minus nn to skip name resolution. For intermittent issues, write to a file with size and count rotation so the disk doesn't fill, then read the file with tcpdump or Wireshark. Capture on both ends to see where packets disappear. Reading the flow: a SYN with no SYN-ACK means blocked or dropped; SYN followed by RST means nothing is listening or a firewall rejects; a complete handshake but no response to the request means the application is slow or hung; repeated retransmissions mean loss; a zero window means the receiver is slow; unexpected RSTs from a different TTL suggest a middlebox. Check timing deltas for latency. Considerations: captures can contain sensitive data, so handle them per policy; overhead on busy hosts; encrypted payloads aren't readable, but handshake and timing are. In Kubernetes use a debug container in the pod's network namespace.`,
    f: ["What does SYN followed by RST indicate?","How do you capture an intermittent problem safely?"]
  },
  108: {
    a: `Fine when idle but failing under load points to a resource that's exhausted only at scale: a finite limit, not a path problem. The prime suspects: the conntrack table full on a NAT gateway, node, or firewall, shown by kernel messages about table full and dropping packets; ephemeral port exhaustion when many connections go to one destination, especially with TIME_WAIT buildup; the listen backlog or accept queue overflowing, shown in the netstat listen overflow counters; file descriptor limits, giving too many open files; connection pool exhaustion in the application or database; NAT gateway port allocation limits; cloud instance network allowances being exceeded, such as packets per second or bandwidth, which providers expose as counters; CPU saturation or softirq overload dropping packets; and downstream rate limits. Diagnosis: capture the exact error and when it begins relative to load, which hints at the threshold; check the counters for each limit at that time; look at kernel logs, ss state counts, pool metrics, and cloud network metrics; and reproduce with a gradual load test while watching the suspects. Fixes follow the limit: raise the limit where sensible, reuse connections, add capacity or spread load, tune queues, or add backpressure. Add alerts for approaching limits, since by the time errors appear, users are affected.`,
    f: ["Which counters reveal a listen queue overflow?","How do cloud network allowance counters show a limit being hit?"]
  },
  109: {
    a: `Kernel network counters, from netstat dash s, nstat, and ss, expose problems that are invisible in application logs. High-value counters: TCP retransmits indicate packet loss or congestion; listen queue overflows and SYNs dropped indicate the accept queue or backlog is too small or the application is too slow to accept, meaning connections are being refused or delayed under load; TCP timeouts and resets show connection failures; out-of-order packets and duplicate ACKs point to reordering or loss; receive buffer pruning and collapsed packets point to memory or buffer pressure from slow consumers; and TIME_WAIT and other state counts show churn or leaks. For interfaces, ip minus s link shows errors, drops, and overruns; ethtool statistics give NIC-level counters such as ring buffer drops; and proc net softnet stat shows kernel backlog drops per CPU. ss with extended information shows per-connection round-trip time, retransmissions, congestion window, and send and receive queues; a growing receive queue means the application isn't reading, and a growing send queue means the peer or network isn't draining. I look at rates over time, not absolute numbers since boot, by sampling twice or exporting to monitoring. Combined, these tell me whether a problem is loss, queueing, a slow application, or a resource limit, and where to look next.`,
    f: ["What does a growing receive queue on a socket mean?","Why look at counter rates instead of totals?"]
  },
  110: {
    a: `Region-specific slowness localises the problem to a path, point of presence, region, or routing decision affecting only those users, so I bisect by geography and layer. First determine scope: is it all users in that region or a subset, perhaps one ISP? A single ISP suggests a peering or transit issue; all users suggest a regional edge or origin problem. Test from that region using synthetic probes or volunteers: mtr and traceroute to the endpoint to see where latency or loss appears, reading mtr correctly. Break down the request timing: DNS resolution, connect, TLS, time to first byte; slow DNS may mean they're sent to a distant or overloaded edge; slow connect indicates network path latency; slow first byte with good connect points to the origin or cache misses. Check CDN behaviour in that region: which POP serves them, the cache hit ratio, and the origin fetch latency. Check for BGP or routing changes, and provider incidents. Look at real user monitoring segmented by region, country, and ISP. Check regional backends: is that region's replica, cache, or dependency slow? Fixes: reroute traffic to a different edge or region, fix cache configuration, add a regional origin or replica, or work with the CDN or ISP with evidence.`,
    f: ["How does real user monitoring help here?","What does slow DNS suggest about a region's problem?"]
  },
  111: {
    a: `TLS failures produce varied and cryptic errors, and openssl s_client is the universal probe to see what the server presents and where the handshake breaks. I connect with the server name flag, because SNI matters: without it the server may present a default certificate. The output shows the certificate chain, the negotiated protocol and cipher, verification results, and the validity dates. What the errors mean: certificate has expired points to expiry or clock skew; unable to get local issuer certificate means the chain is incomplete or the CA isn't trusted by the client; hostname mismatch means the name isn't in the subject alternative names; handshake failure or no shared cipher means a protocol version or cipher mismatch, for example the server only allows TLS 1.3 and the client is old; a certificate unknown or bad certificate alert in mutual TLS means the server rejected the client's certificate; connection reset during the handshake can mean a firewall or middlebox, or a missing SNI. Intermittent failures suggest multiple backends with different certificates, so I test each IP. I can force versions to test compatibility, use the showcerts flag to see the chain, and curl verbose for a client-side view. For deeper analysis, a packet capture shows the TLS alert.`,
    f: ["Why does SNI matter when probing with openssl?","How do you test each backend IP behind a hostname?"]
  },
  112: {
    a: `Server-side request forgery tricks a server into making requests on an attacker's behalf. If an application fetches a user-supplied URL, for example to load an image or call a webhook, an attacker can supply an internal target instead of an external one, and the server, with its network position and trust, performs the request. In the cloud this is especially dangerous because of the instance metadata endpoint, a link-local address that returns instance details and temporary IAM credentials for the attached role. An SSRF that reaches it can leak those credentials, letting the attacker act with the instance's permissions, which was behind several major breaches. Mitigations: for the metadata service, require IMDSv2 on AWS, which needs a session token via a PUT request with a header that simple SSRF can't forge, and limit hop count so containers can't reach it; apply least privilege to instance roles so stolen credentials are limited; and block metadata access for workloads that don't need it. For the application: validate and allow-list destinations, resolve DNS and check the resulting IP against private and link-local ranges, taking care with redirects and DNS rebinding, avoid returning raw responses to the user, and disable unneeded URL schemes.`,
    f: ["Why does IMDSv2 mitigate SSRF?","Why check the resolved IP address and not just the hostname?"]
  },
  113: {
    a: `These three errors each point to a different layer, and distinguishing them narrows the cause immediately. Connection refused means I reached the host, but nothing is listening on that port, and the host actively sent a TCP reset; it's fast. Check that the service is running, listening on the right address and port, with ss, and that a local firewall isn't rejecting. Connection timeout means silence: no response at all, packets are being dropped or not delivered, usually a firewall, security group, network ACL, or route problem, or the host is down; it's slow, since the client waits for retries. Check security groups, flow logs for rejects, routes, and capture packets on both ends. No route to host means the network layer could not find a path or ARP resolution failed: a missing route, a wrong subnet or gateway, an unreachable host on the local segment, or an ICMP host unreachable message from a router; check the routing table, ARP or neighbour state, and the interface configuration. A quick table: refused means the host is up, service is wrong; timeout means something is dropping; no route means no path. Curl or nc show which one. Then fix in the identified layer, and verify from the client side.`,
    f: ["Which of the three is fast and which is slow, and why?","What would you check for a 'no route to host' error?"]
  },
  114: {
    a: `Micro-segmentation limits east-west traffic, the service-to-service flows inside the network, to only what's needed, and egress filtering controls outbound traffic. Together they contain a breach. For micro-segmentation: default-deny between services and explicitly allow required flows; in Kubernetes use NetworkPolicies, or the richer policies of Cilium and Calico, or service mesh authorisation based on workload identity; in VPCs use security groups referencing other groups, and separate subnets and accounts for sensitive tiers. Start in observe mode using flow logs to learn real traffic, then enforce incrementally. Egress filtering matters because compromised workloads depend on outbound connectivity: to download tools, communicate with command-and-control servers, and exfiltrate data. Controls: restrict outbound traffic to known destinations via allow-lists of domains or addresses through a proxy or firewall, DNS filtering, private endpoints instead of internet access for cloud services, and blocking direct internet access from sensitive subnets. Attackers often succeed in getting code execution; egress controls turn a breach into a limited event by preventing data from leaving and stopping lateral tools from phoning home. Operational points: maintain the allow-lists as code, handle updates and package repositories through mirrors, log denied traffic and alert on it, and test that policies work. The trade-off is operational friction when teams need new destinations, so make the request process fast.`,
    f: ["Why is egress filtering valuable even if the attacker gets in?","How do you roll out default-deny network policy safely?"]
  },
  115: {
    a: `Reaching private resources securely has evolved from network-level VPNs and SSH bastions toward identity-based, agent-mediated access with no open inbound ports. A VPN extends the private network to the client, so once connected the user has broad network access; it is over-broad, relies on the you're inside so you're trusted model, and creates a large lateral movement surface, and it needs client software and capacity management. A bastion host is a hardened jump server exposed to the internet that users SSH through to reach private instances; it limits the exposed surface to one host, but needs patching, key management, and access logging, and is a high-value target. Modern alternatives, AWS Systems Manager Session Manager, GCP Identity-Aware Proxy, and Azure Bastion, use an agent on the instance that connects outbound to the service, so there are no inbound ports or public IPs at all; access is controlled by IAM identity and policy, with MFA, short-lived sessions, and full session logging and auditing, and no SSH keys to distribute. For applications, identity-aware proxies give per-application access with device and user context. My preference is the identity-based approach with least privilege, just-in-time access, and audit logs, keeping a VPN or bastion only where needed, and a break-glass path for emergencies.`,
    f: ["Why do SSM or IAP need no open inbound ports?","What is the main weakness of a VPN compared with identity-aware access?"]
  },
  116: {
    a: `Where TLS is decrypted relative to the load balancer determines visibility, performance, and internal security. In termination, or offload, the balancer decrypts traffic and sends plaintext to the backends: the balancer can inspect, route on content, cache, and apply WAF rules, and backends are relieved of TLS work and certificate management. The trade-off is that traffic inside the network is unencrypted, which may not satisfy compliance or a zero-trust posture. In passthrough, the balancer forwards the encrypted bytes at layer four without decrypting: end-to-end encryption is preserved and certificates live only on backends, but the balancer can't see content, so no path routing, header manipulation, or WAF, and the client IP must be conveyed via the PROXY protocol. In re-encryption, or bridging, the balancer terminates the client's TLS, inspects and routes, then opens a new TLS connection to the backend: it gives both layer-seven features and encryption on the internal hop, at the cost of double the TLS processing, more certificates to manage, and added latency. I choose termination for simplicity and performance when the internal network is trusted, passthrough when end-to-end encryption or client certificates must reach the backend, and re-encryption when I need both inspection and internal encryption, as many compliance regimes and zero-trust designs require. Automate certificate lifecycle for all modes.`,
    f: ["When is passthrough the right choice?","What are the costs of re-encryption?"]
  },
  117: {
    a: `Pool exhaustion, with errors like timeout acquiring connection, can come from the pool configuration, the network, or a slow backend, and distinguishing them prevents fixing the wrong layer. The three root causes. First, the pool is too small or leaking: connections aren't returned, for example a missing close in an error path, or the maximum size is too low for the concurrency; the signs are a pool at its maximum with connections held idle and the backend showing few active queries, and leaked connections visible in pool diagnostics that report where they were borrowed. Second, the backend is slow: connections are held longer because queries take longer, so the same pool serves fewer requests per second by Little's Law; the signs are high query latency, database active sessions, locks, and the pool full of connections in use that are busy. Third, the network: connection establishment is slow or connections drop, with retransmissions, resets from idle timeouts, DNS delays, or packet loss; signs include slow connect times and many reconnects. Diagnose by looking at pool metrics, the active, idle, and waiting counts, along with the time connections are held, the backend's own view of sessions, and network statistics.`,
    f: ["How does Little's Law relate to pool size?","Why can enlarging the pool make things worse?"]
  },
  118: {
    a: `ECMP, Equal-Cost Multi-Path routing, spreads traffic across multiple equal-cost paths for bandwidth and redundancy, but per-flow hashing can produce puzzling per-connection differences. The router hashes each flow, typically the five-tuple of source and destination address, ports, and protocol, and picks a path, so packets of one flow stay in order on one path while different flows spread out. Confusing behaviours: a single large transfer is limited to one path's bandwidth, so adding links doesn't speed it up; different connections between the same hosts take different paths, so one works and another fails or is slower if one path has a problem, causing intermittent, per-connection failures that vary with source port; traceroute can show inconsistent paths because probes hash to different next hops; hash collisions send multiple heavy flows to one link while others sit idle; asymmetric paths can break stateful firewalls, because the request and reply hash differently; and when a path is added or removed, the hash redistributes flows and may reset connections unless resilient hashing is used. To debug, vary the source port and test repeatedly to find the bad path, use tools such as paris-traceroute that keep the flow consistent, and check the hash inputs at each device. Mitigate with symmetric hashing, stateful device synchronisation, and spreading flows with more entropy.`,
    f: ["Why does ECMP cause a failure that depends on source port?","What is resilient hashing?"]
  },
  119: {
    a: `Failover that's never tested usually fails when it matters. Designing it means accounting for detection time, convergence, and state, then proving it with drills. Failover time has components: detection, how quickly the failure is noticed, which is the health-check interval times the threshold, or BFD for routing; convergence, how fast traffic actually moves, which depends on DNS TTLs and client caching, BGP convergence, load balancer updates, and connection draining; and recovery of state, such as database promotion and cache warm-up. Design: remove single points of failure at every layer, with redundant links, devices, and zones; use health checks that reflect real service capability; choose fast mechanisms such as load balancers or anycast over slow ones like long-TTL DNS where the objective requires; ensure surviving capacity can carry the load; handle stateful components with replication and tested promotion procedures; and avoid split-brain with quorum and fencing. Verification: run regular failover drills and game days, including killing a zone, a link, or a database primary in production-like conditions, measuring actual detection and recovery time against the RTO and RPO, and watching for hidden dependencies and client behaviour such as stale DNS or non-retrying clients. Automate the failover where possible and keep runbooks for manual steps.`,
    f: ["What are the components of failover time?","Why monitor redundancy itself?"]
  },
  120: {
    a: `This is a capstone design question, and the goal is no single point of failure, secure tiering, and graceful handling of an AZ loss. Per region, span at least two, preferably three, availability zones. At the edge: DNS with health-checked routing, a CDN, and a WAF and DDoS protection. Tier one: public subnets in each AZ holding an internet-facing, multi-AZ layer-seven load balancer, plus one NAT gateway per AZ for outbound access. Tier two: private application subnets in each AZ, running stateless services in an auto scaling group or Kubernetes node groups spread across zones, behind the load balancer, with health checks and connection draining; capacity sized so remaining zones can absorb the loss of one. Tier three: isolated data subnets with no internet route, running a managed database with a multi-AZ standby and automatic failover, read replicas, and a replicated cache; backups to object storage, cross-region where required. Security: security groups chained by tier, so the load balancer reaches the application and only the application reaches the database; network ACLs as a coarse guard; private endpoints for cloud services; TLS everywhere; secrets management; flow logs and centralised logging. Operations: infrastructure as code, monitoring per AZ, and regular AZ failure tests.`,
    f: ["Why one NAT gateway per AZ?","How would you size the application tier for an AZ loss?"]
  },
  121: {
    a: `A hub is a layer one repeater: whatever arrives on one port is copied to every other port, so everyone shares bandwidth and sees everyone's traffic. A switch works at layer two. When a frame arrives, it records the source MAC address against the incoming port in its address table, then looks up the destination MAC and forwards the frame only out of that port. If the destination is unknown or is a broadcast, the switch floods the frame to all ports in the same VLAN. Entries age out after a few minutes. A router works at layer three: it examines the destination IP address, consults its routing table and forwards the packet to the next hop, rewriting the layer two header, and it does not forward broadcasts, so it divides broadcast domains. Layer three switches combine both functions. In the cloud, virtual switches and routers are software, but the same logic applies.`,
    f: ["What happens when the MAC table is full?","What is a broadcast domain?"]
  },
  122: {
    a: `Every TCP or UDP flow can be uniquely identified by five values: the protocol, the source address and port, and the destination address and port. A server listening on port four hundred forty-three can hold hundreds of thousands of simultaneous connections because each client contributes a different source address or port, making every tuple unique. Network devices use the tuple to keep state. A stateful firewall remembers that an outbound flow was allowed and permits the return packets, a NAT gateway maps the internal tuple to an external tuple, a load balancer hashes the tuple to choose a backend consistently, and the kernel's conntrack tracks it. This explains several common problems: a client connecting to the same destination from one source IP can run out of source ports, ECMP hashing can pin a heavy flow to one path, and NAT devices have limited port capacity per external address.`,
    f: ["Why does ECMP pin one flow to one path?","How does this relate to ephemeral port exhaustion?"]
  },
  123: {
    a: `Private ranges are not routable on the public internet, so every organisation reuses them, which is fine until networks must connect. If two VPCs, a data centre and a partner all use ten dot zero dot zero dot zero slash sixteen, peering, VPN and transit gateways cannot route between them without NAT. So I keep a central IP address plan, allocate ranges per environment, region and account with room to grow, and check for overlap automatically in the pipeline. The shared address space one hundred dot sixty-four slash ten is used by service providers for carrier-grade NAT, and it is also handy as a secondary range for container pods, such as with EKS custom networking, because it rarely clashes with corporate ranges. Link-local addresses in one sixty-nine dot two fifty-four slash sixteen are not routed and are used for things like the instance metadata service, which is why securing access to that address matters.`,
    f: ["Why use 100.64.0.0/10 for pods?","How would you check for overlaps automatically?"]
  },
  124: {
    a: `Email authentication is almost entirely published in DNS. MX records list the mail servers that accept mail for the domain, with priorities. An SPF record, a TXT record, lists which IP addresses or providers may send mail using the domain in the envelope sender, and receivers check the connecting IP against it. DKIM has the sending server sign selected headers and the body, adding a signature header, and the receiver fetches the public key from a TXT record under the selector and domain key name to verify it. DMARC builds on both: it requires that the domain in the visible From address aligns with an SPF or DKIM pass, and sets a policy of none, quarantine or reject, plus addresses for aggregate reports. When mail is rejected, I use dig to check each record, watch the SPF ten lookup limit, ensure DKIM keys are published correctly, and begin DMARC in monitoring mode before enforcing.`,
    f: ["What is DMARC alignment?","What happens when SPF exceeds the lookup limit?"]
  },
  125: {
    a: `DNS is a hierarchy, and a zone gets authority over a subdomain through delegation. The parent zone contains NS records naming the authoritative servers for the child. If the name server names are in another domain, a resolver can look those up separately. But if the name servers are inside the delegated zone, such as ns1 dot example dot com for example dot com, resolving the name server requires already knowing where example dot com is served, a circular dependency. Glue records break this loop: the parent, such as the dot com servers, includes the IP addresses of those name servers in its response. If glue is missing, stale or wrong, resolvers can't reach the zone, or reach the old servers after a migration. A lame delegation points to servers that are not authoritative for the zone. I diagnose with dig trace, which follows delegation from the root, and compare NS sets at the parent and child, which must match.`,
    f: ["What is a lame delegation?","Why must parent and child NS sets match?"]
  },
  126: {
    a: `Geographic and latency-based DNS routing choose an answer by estimating where the client is. But the authoritative server only sees the IP address of the recursive resolver that asked, which for a user of a public resolver or a corporate resolver in another country can be far from the user, leading to poor choices. EDNS Client Subnet is an extension where the resolver includes a truncated prefix of the client's address, typically a slash twenty-four, so the authoritative server can pick the closest edge or region. The trade-offs are privacy, since part of the client address is exposed, and cache fragmentation, because answers are cached per subnet. Some resolvers disable it. When troubleshooting why a user is sent to a distant region, I compare answers from different resolvers and test with dig's subnet option. Anycast CDNs reduce reliance on this by routing at the network layer.`,
    f: ["What is the privacy concern with ECS?","How does anycast avoid this problem?"]
  },
  127: {
    a: `DNS uses UDP, which does not verify the source address, so an attacker can send queries with the victim's address forged as the source. The server sends its reply to the victim. Because certain queries, such as those for ANY or DNSSEC-signed records, produce responses many times larger than the request, a small amount of attacker bandwidth is multiplied into a large flood against the victim. To avoid being an amplifier, I make sure recursive resolvers answer only trusted clients and are not open to the internet, enable response rate limiting on authoritative servers so repeated identical responses to one address are throttled, minimise large responses by not answering ANY and keeping records tight, and where I control the network, apply ingress filtering so spoofed packets never leave. As a victim, I rely on upstream DDoS protection, anycast and rate limiting at the edge.`,
    f: ["What is BCP38?","Why is ANY queries special?"]
  },
  128: {
    a: `TCP guarantees delivery by numbering bytes and requiring acknowledgements. If the sender does not see an ACK within a retransmission timeout, derived from measured round-trip time, it resends the data and doubles the timeout each time, so an unlucky segment can stall a connection for seconds. To react faster, if the receiver gets out-of-order data it sends duplicate ACKs, and after three duplicates the sender retransmits the missing segment immediately, which is fast retransmit. Selective acknowledgements add detail in the ACK about which blocks have arrived, so the sender fills only the gaps instead of resending everything after the loss. In a capture, I filter for retransmissions, duplicate ACKs and zero windows. Isolated events are normal, but high rates show packet loss, congestion, a bad link, or an overloaded receiver. Comparing per-path counters tells me whether the problem is local or in the network.`,
    f: ["What triggers a retransmission timeout?","What does a zero window indicate?"]
  },
  129: {
    a: `Nagle's algorithm tries to avoid flooding the network with tiny segments. If there is unacknowledged data in flight, new small writes are buffered until an ACK returns or enough data accumulates to fill a segment. Delayed ACK is the receiver-side optimisation: instead of ACKing every segment immediately, it waits briefly, typically up to forty milliseconds on Linux, hoping to send the ACK along with response data. When an application writes a request in two small pieces, for example a header then a body, the second write waits for an ACK, and the receiver waits to send that ACK, producing a forty millisecond stall on every request. The cure is to write the whole message in one call, or to disable Nagle on the socket with TCP_NODELAY, which most latency-sensitive clients and servers, including many HTTP libraries and databases, do by default. Packet captures show the pause.`,
    f: ["Why does it matter for request-response protocols?","When is Nagle still useful?"]
  },
  130: {
    a: `To use a link fully, a sender must keep enough unacknowledged data in flight to cover the time it takes for the first byte to be acknowledged. That amount is the bandwidth multiplied by the round-trip time. For a one gigabit link with a hundred millisecond RTT, it is about twelve and a half megabytes. The classic TCP window is limited to sixty-four kilobytes, so the window scaling option multiplies it, allowing much larger windows. If socket buffers or the receive window stay small, the throughput ceiling is window divided by RTT, so a long fat network between regions performs badly even though the link is empty. I check that window scaling is enabled, raise the maximum values in the tcp read and write memory settings, let auto-tuning work, use multiple parallel streams for bulk transfers, and consider BBR congestion control. Measuring with iperf3 shows the effect.`,
    f: ["How does BBR help on long links?","Why do parallel streams help?"]
  },
  131: {
    a: `TCP plus TLS needs a transport handshake and then a separate cryptographic handshake before application data flows. QUIC folds them together, so a new connection costs one round trip, and a resumed one can send data in the first flight with zero RTT, with replay caveats. QUIC multiplexes independent streams over a single connection, so packet loss on one stream does not block the others, unlike TCP where a lost segment delays everything behind it. It is implemented in user space over UDP, allowing faster iteration than kernel TCP, and its connection identifier, instead of the address tuple, lets a mobile client move from Wi-Fi to cellular without dropping the connection. HTTP/3 is HTTP over QUIC. Operationally, UDP port four forty-three must be allowed, some networks throttle UDP, load balancers must support it, and observability differs because payloads are encrypted, including most transport headers.`,
    f: ["What is 0-RTT risk?","Why can't middleboxes inspect QUIC easily?"]
  },
  132: {
    a: `OSPF floods link-state information so every router builds the same map of the area and runs shortest path first to compute routes, which gives fast and automatic convergence inside a single administrative domain. It is simple to run in a campus or data centre but does not scale to the internet. BGP is a path-vector protocol where autonomous systems advertise prefixes along with attributes such as AS path, local preference and MED, and policy decides which paths are preferred rather than pure shortest distance. That is why it is the protocol of the internet and of interconnect between organisations. In the cloud I meet BGP everywhere: VPN gateways, Direct Connect, ExpressRoute and Cloud Router exchange routes with on-premises routers over BGP, and Kubernetes CNIs like Calico or Cilium can peer with top-of-rack switches. Within a data centre fabric, many designs now use eBGP throughout instead of OSPF.`,
    f: ["What is the AS path used for?","iBGP versus eBGP?"]
  },
  133: {
    a: `VLANs split a physical switch into multiple logical layer two networks, each its own broadcast domain. When a link has to carry several VLANs, such as between switches or to a hypervisor, it is configured as a trunk, and each frame carries an 802.1Q tag with a VLAN identifier from one to four thousand ninety-four. Access ports carry one VLAN without tags, and the switch adds and removes tags. Because VLANs are separate networks, devices in different VLANs need a router to talk. This is done either with one router interface using sub-interfaces per VLAN, called router on a stick, or more commonly with switch virtual interfaces on a layer three switch, where each VLAN gets a gateway address. Problems come from trunk VLAN lists that omit a VLAN, native VLAN mismatches, and missing gateway configuration. Linux can create VLAN sub-interfaces, and clouds hide this with VPC subnets.`,
    f: ["What is the native VLAN?","Router-on-a-stick versus SVI?"]
  },
  134: {
    a: `Hosts are configured with a single default gateway address, so to survive a router failure, VRRP or Cisco's HSRP lets two routers present one virtual IP and virtual MAC. The elected master owns the address, answers ARP and forwards traffic, and sends periodic advertisements. If the backups stop hearing them for a few intervals, the highest priority backup takes over, and sends a gratuitous ARP so switches and hosts update. Keepalived implements VRRP on Linux for load balancers and gateways. The main failure mode is split brain: if the link carrying the advertisements fails while both nodes remain up, each thinks the other is dead and claims the address, producing duplicate IPs, intermittent connectivity and state confusion. To reduce the risk I use redundant heartbeat paths, authenticate advertisements, tune timers, and add tracking scripts that lower priority if the upstream link or the service is unhealthy.`,
    f: ["What does a gratuitous ARP do on failover?","How would you test failover safely?"]
  },
  135: {
    a: `A router decides in a fixed order. First, longest prefix match: a more specific route such as a slash twenty-four always beats a slash sixteen, whatever its source. If several sources offer the same prefix, such as a static route, OSPF and BGP, the one with the lowest administrative distance is trusted, for example connected routes are zero, static is one, eBGP twenty and OSPF one hundred ten on typical routers. Only when comparing routes from the same protocol does the metric matter. Understanding this order explains surprises like a floating static route, deliberately given a higher distance so it serves as backup only when the dynamic route disappears. Summarisation combines many specific prefixes into one covering prefix when advertising to other routers. It keeps routing tables small and hides instability inside a region, but it can attract traffic for unreachable addresses, creating black holes, if not handled carefully.`,
    f: ["What is a floating static route?","How can summarisation create a black hole?"]
  },
  136: {
    a: `After enabling TLS I first redirect all HTTP requests to HTTPS with a permanent redirect so existing links work. But that first request is still over plain HTTP and vulnerable to interception. HSTS fixes this: the Strict-Transport-Security response header tells the browser to use HTTPS for the domain for max-age seconds, so later visits skip the insecure step and certificate errors cannot be clicked through. Adding includeSubDomains and the preload flag extends this to all subdomains and to browser built-in lists, but these are hard to reverse, so I start with a short max-age, verify every subdomain supports HTTPS, then raise it. Mixed content occurs when an HTTPS page loads scripts, images or frames over HTTP, which browsers block or flag; I fix it by using relative or HTTPS URLs and a Content-Security-Policy with upgrade-insecure-requests during migration. I also check redirect loops caused by TLS termination at a load balancer.`,
    f: ["What does preload do?","How can a redirect loop appear behind a load balancer?"]
  },
  137: {
    a: `All three approaches give near real-time updates but have different shapes. Long polling has the client send a request that the server holds until data is ready, then the client immediately asks again, which works everywhere but is inefficient. Server-Sent Events use one long-lived HTTP response with a text event stream, one way from server to browser, with automatic reconnect built into the browser. WebSockets start as an HTTP request with an Upgrade header, then switch to a persistent bidirectional framed protocol, suitable for chat or collaboration. Operationally, each ties up a connection for a long time, so proxies and load balancers must allow the upgrade headers, set long idle and read timeouts, and avoid buffering responses. Heartbeats or pings keep intermediaries from closing idle connections. Scaling needs attention to connection limits, graceful draining on deploys, and a shared pub/sub layer so a message reaches whichever server holds the recipient's connection.`,
    f: ["Why do connections drop at exactly 60 seconds?","How do you deploy without dropping all sockets?"]
  },
  138: {
    a: `When traffic passes through a reverse proxy or load balancer, the application sees the proxy's address as the peer. For HTTP, proxies add an X-Forwarded-For header listing the original client and each hop, plus X-Forwarded-Proto for the scheme, and the standardised Forwarded header does the same. The application or web server must be configured to trust these headers only when the immediate peer is a known proxy, because anyone can send a forged header. The correct approach is to walk the list from the right, skipping trusted proxy addresses, and take the first untrusted one. For non-HTTP protocols, or TLS passthrough where headers can't be added, the PROXY protocol prefixes the connection with a small header carrying source and destination addresses, supported by HAProxy, NGINX and cloud network load balancers, but both sides must be configured for it or connections break. Network load balancers can also preserve the source IP directly.`,
    f: ["Why take the right-most untrusted entry?","What breaks if only one side enables PROXY protocol?"]
  },
  139: {
    a: `With content negotiation, the client advertises what it can accept, for example gzip, brotli or zstd in Accept-Encoding, and the server picks one and labels the response with Content-Encoding. Text formats like HTML, CSS, JavaScript and JSON shrink by seventy to ninety percent, which reduces latency on slow links, but already compressed formats such as images and video gain nothing. Because the same URL can return different bodies, the server must send Vary: Accept-Encoding so shared caches and CDNs do not serve a compressed body to a client that can't decode it. For static assets I pre-compress at build time with the highest brotli level, for dynamic responses a moderate level balances CPU and size. The security caveat is BREACH, where compression of responses that mix secrets, like CSRF tokens, with attacker-controlled input can reveal the secret through size changes, so I mask tokens or disable compression for sensitive responses.`,
    f: ["What does Vary do?","What is BREACH?"]
  },
  140: {
    a: `IPsec is the interoperable standard. Key exchange with IKE sets up security associations, and ESP encrypts packets in the kernel, which performs well and is what AWS, Azure and Google VPN gateways and most hardware routers speak, so it is the default for site-to-site. It can be complex to configure, with many parameters and NAT traversal quirks. OpenVPN runs in user space over TLS, on UDP or TCP, is highly configurable and traverses restrictive firewalls on port four forty-three, but has higher overhead. WireGuard has a tiny code base, modern fixed cryptography, a simple peer model based on public keys and allowed IP ranges, very fast handshakes and good roaming, and is in the Linux kernel. It lacks built-in user management and dynamic IP assignment, so tools build on top. I choose IPsec for cloud gateways, WireGuard for new overlays, remote access and Kubernetes node encryption, and OpenVPN where compatibility or TCP fallback is needed.`,
    f: ["What does AllowedIPs do in WireGuard?","Why does OpenVPN over TCP perform poorly?"]
  },
  141: {
    a: `In older TLS configurations, the client encrypted a pre-master secret with the server's long-term RSA public key. Anyone who recorded the traffic and later obtained the private key could decrypt every past session. Perfect forward secrecy removes that risk by using ephemeral Diffie-Hellman exchanges: for each connection, both sides generate temporary key pairs, derive a shared secret, and discard the private values afterwards, while the long-term certificate key only authenticates the handshake. Compromise of the server key later does not reveal earlier session keys. In cipher suite names, ECDHE indicates this. TLS 1.3 removed static RSA key exchange entirely and supports only ephemeral key exchange with a small set of strong AEAD ciphers, which is one reason to enable it and disable TLS 1.0 and 1.1. I verify the configuration with scanners and keep session ticket keys rotated, since long-lived ticket keys can undermine forward secrecy.`,
    f: ["Why do session ticket keys matter?","What did TLS 1.3 remove?"]
  },
  142: {
    a: `A certificate can be valid by date but still untrustworthy if its private key leaked. Revocation lets the CA say so. Certificate Revocation Lists are signed files listing revoked serial numbers, which can be large and slow to download. The Online Certificate Status Protocol lets a client query a responder for one certificate, but this adds latency and tells the CA which sites a user visits. OCSP stapling solves both problems: the server periodically fetches a signed response from the CA and sends it during the handshake, so the client gets a fresh status without contacting the CA. An extension called must-staple makes it mandatory. In practice, many browsers soft-fail when they can't check revocation, which weakens the protection, so the industry is moving toward short-lived certificates with automated renewal. When debugging, I check that the server can reach the responder, stapling is enabled and the response is not expired.`,
    f: ["What does must-staple do?","Why do browsers soft-fail?"]
  },
  143: {
    a: `The idea behind a demilitarised zone is that anything exposed to the internet will eventually be attacked, so it should not sit on the same flat network as sensitive systems. Public-facing servers or load balancers live in a separate segment, with firewall rules that allow inbound traffic from the internet only to specific ports, and outbound traffic from the DMZ to the internal network only to the minimum needed, such as the application port to the app tier. The same principle applies in the cloud: public subnets for load balancers, private subnets for application servers, isolated subnets for databases, and security groups that reference each other rather than address ranges. In Kubernetes, network policies segment namespaces, and a service mesh adds identity-based rules. Combined with egress filtering, logging and regular testing, segmentation limits lateral movement after a compromise.`,
    f: ["Why filter egress as well as ingress?","How do security groups referencing groups help?"]
  },
  144: {
    a: `Global server load balancing typically works through DNS. The authority answers with different addresses depending on the user's location, load, or site health, which allows rich policies such as weighted traffic and active-passive failover. Its weaknesses are DNS caching, which delays failover by the TTL and resolver behaviour, and that it sees the resolver rather than the user unless client subnet information is available. Anycast announces the same IP prefix from multiple points of presence, and internet routing delivers each user to the topologically nearest one. If a site fails and stops announcing, traffic shifts as BGP converges, usually within seconds, with no DNS caching issue. It suits stateless or short-lived protocols and edge services such as DNS and CDNs, but long-lived TCP sessions can break if routes shift mid-connection. Many platforms combine anycast edges for entry with application-aware load balancing inside.`,
    f: ["Why can anycast break long TCP sessions?","How do TTLs affect GSLB failover?"]
  },
  145: {
    a: `Opening a new backend connection for each request costs a TCP handshake and possibly TLS, so proxies keep a pool of idle connections to the upstream and reuse them. This reduces latency, backend CPU and ephemeral port usage. The failure mode is a race: if the backend's idle timeout is shorter than the proxy's, the backend closes a pooled connection at the same moment the proxy sends a request on it, and the client gets a 502 or a reset even though both are healthy. This typically appears as rare, intermittent errors on requests that come after a quiet period. The cure is to configure the proxy's keep-alive idle timeout to be shorter than the backend's, enable retries for idempotent requests, and watch metrics for connection resets. Similarly, between client and proxy, the load balancer's idle timeout should be longer than the client's expectations or clients should send heartbeats.`,
    f: ["Why does the race produce 502 errors?","Which side should close idle connections first?"]
  },
  146: {
    a: `Flannel is the simplest: it gives each node a pod subnet and carries traffic between nodes inside a VXLAN overlay, so it works almost anywhere, but it does not enforce network policies on its own and adds encapsulation overhead. Calico routes pod traffic as plain IP, using BGP to advertise pod CIDRs to the fabric or between nodes, which avoids encapsulation and makes pod addresses visible to the network, with an option for VXLAN or IP-in-IP where the underlay does not allow it, and it implements rich network policy using iptables or an eBPF data plane. Cilium builds on eBPF programs in the kernel to forward packets, enforce identity-based policy including layer seven rules, replace kube-proxy with efficient service load balancing, and provide Hubble flow visibility, but requires a recent kernel. The trade-offs are complexity, kernel version requirements, how well each integrates with the cloud provider's native networking, and operational familiarity.`,
    f: ["What does Hubble provide?","When would you prefer BGP routing over an overlay?"]
  },
  147: {
    a: `On modern cloud instances, enhanced networking gives the VM direct access to a virtual function of the physical network card using SR-IOV, bypassing much of the hypervisor's software switching. On AWS this is the Elastic Network Adapter, on Azure Accelerated Networking, and on Google the gVNIC. The benefits are higher bandwidth, higher packets per second, lower latency and less jitter, which matters for databases, trading, storage and HPC. Jumbo frames raise the MTU from fifteen hundred to nine thousand, so each packet carries more payload and CPU overhead drops, helping bulk transfers inside a VPC or between peered VPCs in the same region. However, the path must support the larger size end to end, and traffic leaving through the internet gateway, VPN or some peering paths is limited to a smaller MTU, so path MTU discovery must work or MSS must be clamped. I verify the driver, enable the features, and test with don't-fragment pings.`,
    f: ["Why do packets per second limits matter?","How would you validate jumbo frame support?"]
  },
  148: {
    a: `Partners often restrict access to a list of source IP addresses. In a private network, outbound internet traffic is source-NATed anyway, so I control the address by controlling the NAT. In AWS, a NAT gateway with an Elastic IP in each availability zone gives stable addresses, in Google Cloud a Cloud NAT with reserved static addresses, and in Azure a NAT gateway with static public IPs. For Kubernetes, pod traffic leaves with the node address or the NAT address, so I can restrict egress to nodes in a specific subnet, or use an egress gateway feature in Cilium or Calico to route selected pods through a few gateway nodes with fixed IPs. Considerations are availability, since one gateway is a single point of failure, port exhaustion, the cost of NAT data processing, and documenting the IP set for partners. An alternative is a managed egress proxy or private connectivity with PrivateLink style endpoints.`,
    f: ["Why does each AZ need its own NAT?","What is an egress gateway in Cilium?"]
  },
  149: {
    a: `SERVFAIL means the resolver could not obtain a valid answer. Since another resolver succeeds, I look for differences. First I run dig with trace to follow delegation from the root and see whether the parent and child name servers agree and whether all are reachable. A lame or unreachable authoritative server may be tolerated by one resolver and not another. Then DNSSEC: if the zone is signed and has an expired signature or a mismatched DS record at the parent, validating resolvers return SERVFAIL, while non-validating ones answer normally. Testing with the checking disabled flag reveals this, and delv shows the validation chain. Other causes are firewalls dropping fragmented UDP responses or EDNS packets, so the resolver should fall back to TCP, and resolver cache or rate limits. The fix depends on the cause: repair the delegation or DNSSEC at the zone owner, allow TCP port fifty-three and large packets, or temporarily add a negative trust anchor while waiting.`,
    f: ["What is a DS record?","What is a negative trust anchor?"]
  },
  150: {
    a: `The pattern of errors after a quiet spell, affecting only some requests, strongly suggests the keep-alive race. The proxy keeps a pool of idle connections to the backend. If the backend, or a firewall or NAT in between, closes one that has been idle longer than its timeout, the proxy may not notice yet, sends a POST on the dead connection, and gets a reset. Idempotent requests such as GET are usually retried automatically on a new connection, so users see nothing, but proxies do not retry POST by default because it might duplicate side effects, so the 502 is returned. I confirm with the proxy error log messages and a packet capture showing the reset on a reused connection. The fix is to make the proxy's idle timeout shorter than the backend's and any network device's, enable TCP keepalives, and for APIs designed with idempotency keys, enable retries for POST safely.`,
    f: ["How do idempotency keys make retries safe?","What does a NAT idle timeout do?"]
  },
  151: {
    a: `I start by getting precise information: what error do they see, since a timeout, a certificate error and a wrong page all point to different layers, which ISP and resolver they use, and whether it affects IPv4, IPv6 or both. DNS is the first check, because an ISP resolver may return stale or wrong answers or fail on DNSSEC. Next, I test both address families, since a broken IPv6 route with an AAAA record can cause long delays or failures while IPv4 works. I look at path MTU, because tunnels and PPPoE links reduce MTU and black holes only affect larger packets, such as the TLS server hello. A routing problem, like a BGP leak or a missing route from that provider's network, shows in traceroutes from their side, so I use public looking glasses and distributed probing services to test from within that network. Depending on findings, I fix DNS, MTU or address announcements, or engage the provider.`,
    f: ["How does a broken AAAA record appear to users?","What is a looking glass?"]
  },
  152: {
    a: `A SYN flood sends many connection requests without completing the handshake, filling the server's queue of half-open connections so legitimate clients can't connect. I confirm by counting sockets in SYN_RECV state, watching listen overflow and SYN cookie counters in nstat, and sampling packets to see a high rate of SYNs, often from spoofed or widely distributed addresses, with few completed connections. Mitigations at the host are enabling SYN cookies, which let the server encode state in the sequence number and avoid allocating memory until the client replies with a valid ACK, raising the backlog and somaxconn, and shortening SYN-ACK retries. At the network edge I rate-limit SYNs per source, use cloud DDoS protection, a CDN or a load balancer that proxies TCP and absorbs the handshake, and filter obviously spoofed ranges. Afterwards I review logs, tune alerts on SYN_RECV counts, and rehearse the response.`,
    f: ["How do SYN cookies work?","Why is spoofing hard to filter?"]
  },
  153: {
    a: `When two hosts use the same IP, ARP replies conflict, so other machines keep switching which MAC address they send to, and connections work for a while and then reset. Operating systems often log a duplicate address warning. To confirm, I use arping in duplicate detection mode, look at the neighbour table to see whether the MAC address for that IP changes, and capture ARP traffic to see which two MACs answer. On a managed switch I look up those MACs in the forwarding table to find which ports they are on, or in virtual environments query the hypervisor. Typical causes are a DHCP scope overlapping with manually assigned static addresses, a cloned virtual machine template keeping its static configuration, or a failover pair both active. The fix is correcting the address assignment, excluding static ranges from DHCP, and using IP address management to prevent recurrence. Port security or ARP inspection can alert on conflicts.`,
    f: ["What does arping -D do?","What is dynamic ARP inspection?"]
  },
  154: {
    a: `An established tunnel just means both ends negotiated keys. Data flow depends on several other things. First, routing: each side needs a route for the remote subnet pointing into the tunnel, and the return path must come back through the tunnel too. If return traffic takes a different route, such as the default route out to the internet, the remote end never sees replies. Second, for policy-based IPsec, the traffic selectors, which define the local and remote subnets that are encrypted, must match on both ends exactly, otherwise packets in one direction match and the other do not. Third, firewalls and security groups must allow traffic in both directions, including stateful tracking, and NAT rules may rewrite source addresses so the other side doesn't recognise them. Overlapping address ranges and MTU or fragmentation issues also cause partial flows. I capture traffic on both sides to see where packets disappear, then correct routing or selectors.`,
    f: ["What are traffic selectors?","Policy-based versus route-based VPN?"]
  },
  155: {
    a: `A fast link does not guarantee a fast single connection. Throughput per TCP flow is bounded by the window size divided by round-trip time, so on a hundred millisecond path with a default buffer a single stream may reach only tens of megabits. I measure with iperf3, first with one stream and then with several in parallel. If parallel streams scale well, the bottleneck is per-connection: window scaling disabled, socket buffers too small, or slow congestion control recovering from packet loss. I check the connection's RTT, congestion window and retransmission count with ss. High loss on a long path hurts classic loss-based algorithms severely, so I may enable BBR and increase the maximum buffer sizes. For real workloads, I use tools that open many parallel streams or chunk uploads, such as multipart transfers to object storage, and check that cloud limits like per-flow throughput caps are not the issue.`,
    f: ["Why is cloud per-flow throughput limited?","What does BBR change?"]
  },
  156: {
    a: `When a proxy terminates the client connection, the backend's socket peer is the proxy, so any feature that uses the remote address, logging, geo-location, allowlists and rate limiting, sees one IP for all users and may block everyone after a few requests. The proxy passes the original address in X-Forwarded-For or via the PROXY protocol. I configure the web server or framework to read it, but only when the direct peer is within the proxy's address ranges, and to take the right-most address that is not itself a trusted proxy. I then rate-limit based on that real client IP. I check what the load balancer actually sends by echoing headers, and make sure multiple proxy layers such as a CDN and a load balancer are all included in the trusted list. Finally, I test spoofing: sending a forged header directly should not change the identified client, otherwise attackers can evade limits.`,
    f: ["Why only trust from known proxy ranges?","How do multiple proxy layers affect the header?"]
  },
  157: {
    a: `curl works because CORS is a browser-enforced policy, so the server may be responding perfectly well but the browser refuses to expose the response to the page's JavaScript. For a cross-origin request with non-simple methods or headers, the browser first sends a preflight OPTIONS request with the origin, method and headers it wants to use. The server must respond with the matching Access-Control-Allow-Origin, Allow-Methods and Allow-Headers headers and a success status. If the request includes cookies or authorisation, the response must also include Allow-Credentials true and a specific origin, not a wildcard. I reproduce the preflight with curl to see exactly what headers come back, and check that proxies, API gateways or authentication layers aren't blocking OPTIONS before it reaches the app. The fix is configuring an explicit allowlist of origins, caching preflights with Max-Age, and ensuring error responses also carry the CORS headers so the browser shows the real error.`,
    f: ["Why does a wildcard origin fail with credentials?","What is a simple request?"]
  },
  158: {
    a: `A drop at a precise round number such as sixty seconds is a timer, not randomness. Many proxies and cloud load balancers close connections that have had no data for sixty seconds by default, and WebSockets can stay quiet while waiting for user events. I identify which hop closes it by checking each layer's idle timeout: the CDN, the cloud load balancer, a reverse proxy like NGINX with its proxy read timeout, an ingress controller, and NAT gateways with their own connection tracking timeouts. The fix is twofold. First, send heartbeats, either WebSocket ping frames or small application messages, at an interval shorter than the shortest timeout, perhaps every twenty to thirty seconds, which also detects dead peers. Second, raise the timeouts where appropriate and ensure the upgrade headers are passed. The client should also reconnect with exponential backoff and resume state, because connections will eventually drop during deployments.`,
    f: ["Why are ping frames better than app messages?","How should clients reconnect?"]
  },
  159: {
    a: `Kubernetes Services using kube-proxy balance at the connection level. A gRPC client opens one HTTP/2 connection and multiplexes all its requests over it, so after the first connection is established to one pod, new pods added later receive nothing from that client, and a few busy clients can overload a single pod. The fix is to balance at the request level. Options include putting an L7 proxy such as Envoy or a service mesh in the data path, which understands HTTP/2 streams and spreads them across backends, using client-side load balancing with a headless Service so the client resolves all pod IPs and uses a policy like round robin, or an ingress controller with gRPC support. I can also configure the server with a maximum connection age and grace period, so clients reconnect periodically and rediscover new pods. Health checking and graceful shutdown remain important in either approach.`,
    f: ["What is a headless Service?","Why does MaxConnectionAge help?"]
  },
  160: {
    a: `A 429 is a request to slow down, so blind immediate retries make the overload worse and can turn a limit into an outage through a retry storm. The client should read the Retry-After header or rate-limit headers and wait at least that long. Where there is no hint, use exponential backoff with jitter, so many clients don't retry in lockstep, plus a maximum number of attempts and a total deadline. Only idempotent operations should be retried automatically, or those protected by idempotency keys. Better still is prevention: a client-side token bucket that keeps request rates under the known limit, request coalescing and caching, and a circuit breaker that stops calling the dependency for a while when failures are high. Retries should be implemented at only one layer of the call chain, otherwise they multiply. I monitor the 429 rate, queue depth and retry counts, and negotiate higher limits or use batch endpoints when legitimate demand exceeds quota.`,
    f: ["Why add jitter?","What is retry amplification?"]
  },
  161: {
    a: `First I measure the impact instead of guessing. I enable logging of the negotiated TLS version and cipher with the user agent for successful connections, and count handshake failures in the load balancer or proxy error logs, which show alerts such as protocol version or no shared cipher. That reveals which client populations are affected, whether browsers, mobile apps, monitoring probes or embedded devices. I reproduce with openssl s_client forcing specific protocol versions and ciphers. Then I decide: modern guidance is TLS 1.2 minimum with strong AEAD ciphers and TLS 1.3 enabled, so if the failures come from a handful of old integrations, I ask the owners to upgrade, with a deadline. If a business-critical legacy client must be supported, I isolate it on a separate hostname or listener with a weaker policy and compensating controls, rather than weakening the main endpoint. I document the policy and review it regularly.`,
    f: ["Why isolate legacy clients on a separate endpoint?","What does 'no shared cipher' mean?"]
  },
  162: {
    a: `With HTTPS, the server must present its certificate before the HTTP request is sent, so it can't use the Host header to choose it. Server Name Indication solves this by having the client include the hostname in the TLS ClientHello. The server or load balancer selects the matching certificate, and then routes the HTTP request by Host header. If I connect by IP address, or use a name not configured on the server, there is no matching SNI, so the server falls back to its default certificate and default virtual host, which is often another site. To debug I use openssl s_client with and without the servername option and compare, and curl with the resolve option to connect to a specific IP while sending the correct name. I then check the default server configuration, the certificate list on the load balancer listener, wildcard coverage, and whether a CDN origin is configured to send the right SNI.`,
    f: ["What happens without SNI?","Why does curl --resolve help?"]
  },
  163: {
    a: `Credential stuffing uses leaked username and password pairs from other breaches, spread across many IP addresses to evade simple limits. I start by confirming the pattern in logs: a high volume of login attempts, a large share of failures, many different usernames per source and often a user agent or timing pattern. At the edge I use a WAF with rate-based rules scoped to the login path, managed bot control or CAPTCHA challenges, and temporary blocks on abusive networks or countries that are not part of our users, balancing false positives. In the application I throttle by account as well as by address, add progressive delays, require multi-factor authentication or step-up verification for risky logins, check passwords against breached lists, and avoid revealing whether a username exists. I also protect with alerts on failure ratios, notify affected users, and confirm the capacity of downstream systems, such as the identity database, during an attack.`,
    f: ["Why is per-IP limiting not enough?","What is step-up authentication?"]
  },
  164: {
    a: `With a single routing table and a single default gateway, a host answers every request through the default route's interface. If a request arrives on the second interface, the reply goes out the first, with a source address belonging to the second network. A stateful firewall or the upstream router sees a response to a connection it never saw, or a spoofed-looking source, and drops it. The server's own reverse path filter may also drop incoming packets whose best return path is another interface. The fix is policy routing: create a separate routing table for each interface, with its own default gateway, and add rules that select the table based on the source address, so replies leave the way requests came in. Persist it in the network configuration, relax rp_filter to loose mode if needed, and verify with ip route get using the from option and with packet captures on both interfaces.`,
    f: ["What does rp_filter do?","How does ip rule differ from ip route?"]
  },
  165: {
    a: `If a corporate network uses part of the range that Docker assigns to its default bridge or to user-defined networks, the host's routing table sends packets for those addresses to the local bridge instead of the real network, so containers and sometimes the host itself cannot reach those destinations. I inspect the routing table to find the conflicting route and the Docker network configuration. For Docker, I set the bridge IP and default address pools in the daemon configuration to unused ranges and recreate the networks, which requires restarting containers. For Kubernetes, the pod CIDR and service CIDR are set at cluster creation and are difficult to change, so the right approach is prevention: coordinate with the network team, choose ranges that do not overlap with on-premises, VPN partners or other clusters, record them in an address management system, and consider using non-routable ranges such as the carrier grade NAT block for pods.`,
    f: ["Can you change a Kubernetes pod CIDR later?","Why record ranges in IPAM?"]
  },
  166: {
    a: `I start by measuring: VPC flow logs or cloud network metrics aggregated by source and destination zone, and request latency broken down by calling zone, show which service pairs generate cross-zone traffic and how much it costs. Typical sources are random load balancing across all zones, replicas not evenly spread, database replicas, and chatty protocols. To reduce it, I enable topology-aware routing for Kubernetes services so traffic prefers endpoints in the caller's zone, use zone-aware load balancing in the mesh or ingress with locality settings, place tightly coupled services together, cache frequently read data, batch calls and compress payloads. I must keep enough capacity in each zone, because strict affinity can overload a small zone, and keep failover to other zones when local endpoints are unhealthy. After changes I recheck both cost and latency, and confirm the availability design still tolerates the loss of a zone.`,
    f: ["How does topology-aware routing decide?","What are the risks of zone affinity?"]
  },
  167: {
    a: `In TCP, each side advertises how much more data it can accept, the receive window. A zero window announcement means the receiver's socket buffer is full because the application has not read the data yet, so the sender must stop and send only occasional probes. Since the network is not the bottleneck here, tuning the network will not help. I look at the receiving host: the Recv-Q column in ss shows bytes waiting in the buffer for a particular connection. Then I examine why the application is slow to read: a saturated CPU or thread pool, a blocking call such as a slow database or disk write in the read loop, garbage collection pauses, lock contention, or a single-threaded design. Increasing socket buffers can smooth short bursts but only hides a persistent slow consumer. The real fix is making the consumer faster, adding parallelism or backpressure, or reducing the data rate.`,
    f: ["What is Recv-Q?","What are window probes?"]
  },
  168: {
    a: `If only some nodes fail, something differs between them. I run the same debug container pinned to a working node and a failing node and compare results, checking DNS resolution first and then the TCP connection. Differences usually come from placement: nodes in different subnets or availability zones may use different route tables, NAT gateways or network ACLs, and the partner may have allowlisted only some NAT addresses. Security groups attached to node groups can differ, a node might have a stale or missing SNAT rule in the CNI configuration, or a host firewall may block it. I check the route taken from the node, the outbound IP seen by an external echo service, and node labels for zone and group. Once I identify the difference, I fix the routing or allowlist, and prevent drift by building node groups from the same templates and testing egress as part of cluster validation.`,
    f: ["How can you see the node's outbound IP?","Why would a CNI SNAT rule go missing?"]
  },
  169: {
    a: `Intermittent resolution failures under load often come from limits rather than from broken configuration. In the cloud, the VPC resolver is rate limited per network interface, for example around one thousand packets per second in AWS, and bursts of lookups, made worse by search domain expansion where one name generates several queries, exceed it and queries are dropped. UDP drops from conntrack table exhaustion or a congested NIC have the same symptom, as does an overloaded in-cluster resolver. I measure query rates and timeouts, test lookups repeatedly against the resolver, and check drop counters. Fixes include deploying a node-local caching resolver, which absorbs repeated queries, lowering ndots or using fully qualified names, increasing cache TTLs and using HTTP connection reuse so applications resolve less often, spreading load across multiple resolvers, and tuning resolver timeout and attempts settings. Monitoring DNS error rates and latency gives early warning.`,
    f: ["What is search domain expansion?","What does a node-local DNS cache do?"]
  },
  170: {
    a: `I use two complementary approaches. Static analysis checks the configuration: list security groups, network ACLs, route tables, public IP assignments and, for Kubernetes, network policies, and flag anything allowing zero dot zero dot zero dot zero slash zero or broad ranges to database ports. Infrastructure as code scanners run in the pipeline to prevent such rules from ever being deployed. Reachability tools such as AWS Reachability Analyzer, Azure Network Watcher or Google Connectivity Tests evaluate whether a path exists between a source and destination for a given port, and produce evidence of which component allows or blocks it. Active testing then confirms reality: authorised port scans from the internet and from the web tier show which ports respond, and I compare them with the intended matrix. I document the expected flows, the evidence, test dates and owners, schedule the checks to repeat, and alert on drift. This turns a one-time audit into continuous assurance.`,
    f: ["Why combine static and active testing?","How would you keep this evidence current?"]
  }
};
